    35  \title{A fitness-independent family of simultaneous amplifiers beyond relative fitness $3/2$}
    36  \author{Alec Kriebel\\
    37  Independent Researcher\\
    38  \href{mailto:me@aleckriebel.com}{me@aleckriebel.com}\\
    39  \href{https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X}{ORCID: 0009-0001-9320-500X}}
    40  \date{August 22, 2026}
    41  
    42  \begin{document}
    43  \maketitle
    44  
    45  \begin{abstract}
    46  We study fixation from a uniformly random single mutant on finite connected
    47  loopless undirected weighted graphs under both Birth--death (Bd) and
    48  death--Birth (dB) Moran updating.  Let $\Rsim$ be the supremum of the
    49  fitness intervals $(1,R)$ on which one graph family, chosen independently
    50  of fitness, eventually amplifies fixation under both rules.  Let $\Rhyb$ be
    51  the unique root in $(3/2,151/100)$ of
    52  \[
    53   R^6-8R^5+22R^4-30R^3+21R^2-6R+1.
    54  \]
    55  We construct one fitness-independent family that simultaneously amplifies
    56  every fixed $1<r<\Rhyb$ for all sufficiently large members of the sequence.
    57  Therefore
    58  $\Rsim\geq\Rhyb=1.5028569127905696\ldots>3/2$.
    59  
    60  The construction combines a large clique with dilute hub pendants and
    61  dilute, internally heavy two-vertex satellites.  An exact finite-state
    62  derivation of the weak-cut limiting trace, together with uniform establishment, cleanup, and
    63  reciprocal-invasion estimates at the scale of the eventual gain, reduces the two
    64  normalized fixation corrections to explicit response functions.  Optimizing
    65  their common positivity interval yields the displayed sextic.  The same
    66  analysis proves that $\Rhyb$ is optimal among fixed positive
    67  $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute pair--pendant response
    68  model; a specialization with entirely rational edge weights already crosses
    69  $3/2$.  Thus $3/2$ is not the exact simultaneous-amplification threshold.
    70  The unrestricted value of $\Rsim$, and even a finite universal upper bound,
    71  remain open.
    72  \end{abstract}
    73  
    74  \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} evolutionary graph theory; Moran process;
    75  fixation probability; simultaneous amplification; Birth--death updating;
    76  death--Birth updating.
    77  
    78  \medskip
    79  \noindent\textbf{Mathematics Subject Classification (2020):}
    80  92D15, 60J10, 05C81.
    81  
    82  \section{Introduction}
    83  
    84  Evolutionary graph theory extends the finite-population Moran process by
    85  representing sites as vertices and replacement opportunities as weighted
    86  edges \citep{Moran1958,Lieberman2005,Allen2017}.  The order of the two elementary Moran events matters:
    87  stars and many other structures that amplify selection when birth precedes
    88  death suppress it when death precedes birth
    89  \citep{Hindersin2015,Allen2020}.  Random edge weights also tend to make
    90  undirected networks less amplifying, and often suppressing, which emphasizes
    91  that useful weighting is a design problem rather than a generic effect
    92  \citep{Bhaumik2024}.  Death--Birth amplification is necessarily
    93  transient in fitness on every fixed noncomplete strongly connected structure
    94  \citep{Tkadlec2020}, and transient dB amplifiers have also been sought through
    95  guided spectral edge deletion \citep{Richter2023}.  A distinct approach
    96  randomizes the update order within a single mixed Moran process
    97  \citep{Brewster2026}.  Here robustness instead means that the same graph
    98  beats the well-mixed benchmark under each pure rule separately.  These
    99  tensions make robustness to update order a natural design problem rather
   100  than an automatic consequence of ordinary amplification.
   101  
   102  A population structure is a \emph{simultaneous amplifier} at fitness $r$
   103  when it raises uniformly initialized fixation probability, relative to the
   104  complete graph of the same order, under both Bd and dB updating.  The
   105  asymptotic problem has the quantifier order
   106  \[
   107   \exists\{G_t\}\ \forall r\in(1,R)\ \exists t_0(r)\
   108   \forall t\geq t_0(r).
   109  \]
   110  The graphs and weights may depend on $t$, but not on $r$.
   111  
   112  A complementary fixed-graph obstruction has the opposite quantifier order.
   113  No fixed finite weighted structure is a strict dB amplifier for every
   114  $r>1$: every structure not dB-equivalent to the complete graph is suppressing
   115  above a graph-dependent threshold $R_G$, whereas the equivalent class ties
   116  the baseline \citep{Kriebel2026fixed}.  This
   117  $\forall G\,\exists R_G$ statement supplies no graph-uniform threshold and
   118  therefore does not rule out the present
   119  $\exists\{G_t\}\,\forall r<R\,\exists t_0(r)$ order; the thresholds
   120  $R_{G_t}$ may vary with $t$.
   121  
   122  \citet{Svoboda2024} constructed simultaneous amplifiers on $1<r<1.2$.
   123  A preliminary unrefereed research release established $\Rsim\geq3/2$ with a
   124  center--triangle family, while that particular family suppresses at its
   125  endpoint \citep{Kriebel2026half}.  The present manuscript supersedes that
   126  release in its lower-bound conclusion by crossing $3/2$.
   127  The numerical increment is small, but the conclusion is qualitative: the
   128  earlier endpoint is not the unrestricted phase boundary.
   129  
   130  The proof has four ingredients.  First, a finite Schur complement gives the
   131  rare-migration trace without an independence or branching approximation.
   132  Second, stopped birth--death comparisons control establishment and the
   133  entire path to fixation at the smaller $q/C$ scale of the final gain.  Third,
   134  the exact two-coordinate macro chain retains adverse reversals while deriving
   135  the pair gate.  Finally, optimizing the two resulting response functions
   136  produces an algebraic tangency and the degree-six polynomial in the abstract.
   137  This also proves fixed-parameter optimality within the displayed first-order
   138  pair--pendant response model.  No universal upper bound is inferred; the
   139  unrestricted value of $\Rsim$ remains open.
   140  
