     1	# Independent audit: response functions, optimization, and quantifier transfer
     2	
     3	**Checkpoint:** 2026-08-22 12:40 PDT  
     4	**Auditor:** response/quantifier subagent  
     5	**Best-guess completion:** **100% of the assigned response-function, optimization, rational-edge, and quantifier-transfer slice.**  
     6	**Boundary:** read-only review of `delivered_copy`; no network, contact, upload, commit, push, or modification of delivered material. The independent exact calculation is `independent_checks/response_math/independent_response_audit.py`. It does not import the authors' programs.
     7	
     8	## Bottom line
     9	
    10	I found **no critical, high, medium, or low mathematical defect in the audited algebra and quantifier chain**. Conditional on the weak-cut trace and center-module asymptotics supplied by Propositions 5 and 6, the gate ratios, both first-order response functions, their common feasibility gap, the sextic tangency, fixed-positive-parameter optimality, rational-edge threshold, and final diagonal transfer all check exactly.
    11	
    12	The strongest independently verified statement in this slice is:
    13	
    14	> If the compact-uniform asymptotics in Propositions 5, 6, and 12 hold, then the manuscript's explicitly defined, fitness-independent graph sequence has both normalized fixation gains positive for every fixed $1<r<R_{\rm hyb}$ once $t$ is sufficiently large; the exact response-model obstruction also prevents any fixed real σ, and hence any fixed positive σ, from extending a response-positive interval immediately past $R_{\rm hyb}$.
    15	
    16	This does **not** independently certify the long stopped-process/expectation/logarithmic estimates underlying Proposition 6. Those are being audited separately. I treated them as hypotheses here and checked that the response proof uses them consistently and at the claimed error scale.
    17	
    18	## Exact source anchors
    19	
    20	| Component | PDF location | LaTeX source location |
    21	|---|---|---|
    22	| Update rules, uniform initialization, complete-graph baselines, and $R_{\rm sim}$ quantifiers | p. 3, equations (1)--(6), Definition 1 | `main.tex:141-199` |
    23	| Sextic, $\sigma_*$, $\lambda_*$, graph parameters, and fitness-independent weak cut | pp. 3--4, equations (7)--(8), Lemma 2 | `main.tex:203-225`, `276-322` |
    24	| Main amplification statement | p. 5, Theorem 3; proof on p. 18 | `main.tex:324-336`, `1348-1357` |
    25	| Separated finite trace and weak-cut limit | pp. 5--6, Proposition 5, equations (9)--(10) | `main.tex:379-440` |
    26	| Center-module inputs | p. 6, Proposition 6, equations (11)--(15) | `main.tex:445-466` |
    27	| Portal functionals, gate-rate table, adverse reversals, and $Z_B,Z_D$ | pp. 16--17, Proposition 12 | `main.tex:1144-1247`, especially `1178-1179`, `1193-1205`, `1214-1245` |
    28	| Two response functions and component simplifications | p. 17, Proposition 13, equations (41)--(44) | `main.tex:1249-1290`, especially `1261-1268`, `1280-1288` |
    29	| Feasibility, tangency, sextic, and monotonicity | pp. 17--18, Lemma 14 | `main.tex:1292-1346` |
    30	| Fixed-parameter response optimality | p. 18, Proposition 15 | `main.tex:1359-1382` |
    31	| Rational-edge specialization | pp. 18--19, Corollary 16 | `main.tex:1384-1416` |
    32	| Scope limitations and eventual-only nature of the theorem | p. 19, Discussion | `main.tex:1420-1465` |
    33	
    34	## 1. Baselines and first-order bookkeeping
    35	
    36	### Complete-graph baselines: verified
    37	
    38	For Bd on (K_n), the down/up odds of the mutant-count chain are (1/r), so the standard product-of-odds solution gives
    39	
    40	\[
    41	 \rho_{\rm Bd}(K_n,r)=\frac{1-r^{-1}}{1-r^{-n}}.
    42	\]
    43	
    44	For dB, the one-step odds are not constant. At mutant count (k), direct cancellation gives
    45	
    46	\[
    47	 \gamma_k=\frac{n-1+(r-1)k}
    48	 {r\{n-1+(r-1)(k-1)\}},
    49	 \qquad
    50	 \prod_{j=1}^k\gamma_j
    51	 =r^{-k}\frac{n-1+(r-1)k}{n-1}.
    52	\]
    53	
    54	Substitution in the exact birth-death hitting formula telescopes to
    55	
    56	\[
    57	 \rho_{\rm dB}(K_n,r)
    58	 =\frac{n-1}{n}\frac{1-r^{-1}}{1-r^{-(n-1)}}.
    59	\]
    60	
    61	These agree with PDF equations (5)--(6), `main.tex:179-189`. The independent script also solved these product formulas exactly for (2\le n\le12) at three rational fitnesses.
    62	
    63	On every fixed compact (r\in[r_-,r_+]\Subset(1,\infty)), with (n\asymp C=t^4),
    64	
    65	\[
    66	 \rho_{\rm Bd}(K_n,r)-p=O(r_-^{-n}),\qquad
    67	 \rho_{\rm dB}(K_n,r)-p=O(C^{-1}),\qquad p=1-r^{-1}.
    68	\]
    69	
    70	Because $\eta:=q/C=t^{-3}$, both are $o(\eta)$. Thus replacing the finite baselines by $p$ in the first-order derivation on p. 17 is legitimate. Positivity is also clear: $r>1$ makes $p$, $1-r^{-n}$, and $1-r^{-(n-1)}$ positive.
    71	
    72	### Pair and pendant components: verified from the stated asymptotics
    73	
    74	For an isolated (K_2), a single mutant fixes with probability (r/(r+1)) under Bd. Under dB, the first uniformly selected death decides the outcome because the remaining vertex is the only possible parent, so the singleton value is exactly (1/2), independent of (r). This verifies `main.tex:1207-1212`.
    75	
    76	The finite trace, PDF equation (9), is
    77	
    78	\[
    79	 \rho_U^0=\frac{C+m}{N}a_H^UP_U^H
    80	 +\frac{2q}{N}a_P^UP_U^P,
    81	 \qquad N=C+m+2q.
    82	\]
    83	
    84	Set (m/C=\lambda\eta+o(\eta)). Proposition 6 gives center numerators
    85	
    86	\[
    87	 \frac{C+m}{C}\,a_H^{\rm Bd}=p+\lambda\eta+o(\eta),
    88	 \qquad
    89	 \frac{C+m}{C}\,a_H^{\rm dB}=p+o(\eta).
    90	\]
    91	
    92	Together with (P_U^H=1-o(\eta)), the first-order normalized traces are therefore
    93	
    94	\[
    95	 \frac{p+\lambda\eta+2\eta\,[r/(r+1)]\,Z_B/(1+Z_B)}
    96	 {p\{1+(\lambda+2)\eta\}}+o(\eta)
    97	\]
    98	
    99	for Bd and
   100	
   101	\[
   102	 \frac{p+2\eta\,[1/2]\,Z_D/(1+Z_D)}
   103	 {p\{1+(\lambda+2)\eta\}}+o(\eta)
   104	\]
   105	
   106	for dB. Differentiating these rational expressions at η(=0) independently reproduces equations (41)--(42).
   107	
   108	The pendant contribution is exactly
   109	
   110	\[
   111	 \lambda(1/p-1)=\frac{\lambda}{r-1}
   112	\]
   113	
   114	under Bd and (-\lambda) under dB. The (-2) inside each pair correction comes from the two pair vertices displaced in the vertex-count denominator. This accounts for every first-order component; there is no missing baseline or population-size term.
   115	
   116	## 2. Gate odds and macro rates
   117	
   118	The portal identities on p. 16 are exact:
   119	
   120	\[
   121	 I_P=2/W=2\sigma/C,\qquad J_P=1/W=\sigma/C.
   122	\]
   123	
   124	The second identity uses two dB singleton committors of $1/2$. Applying the Proposition 6 limits to the $A,D$ columns of the gate table gives
   125	
   126	| Rule | $A$, pair invades center | $D$, pair is lost | $A/D$ |
   127	|---|---:|---:|---:|
   128	| Bd | $CrI_Pp\to2\sigma(r-1)$ | $2I_H/(r+1)\to2/(r+1)$ | $Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1)$ |
   129	| dB | $2rJ_H(r)\to2(r-1)$ | $(C/r)J_P(1/r)=\sigma/r$ | $Z_D=2r(r-1)/\sigma$ |
   130	
   131	This verifies the macro-rate formulas at `main.tex:1178-1179` and `1241-1244` independently of the supplied coefficient certifier.
   132	
   133	The global-sweep use of adverse reversals is also consistent. From ((1,k)), the probability that the center reverses before the next successful pair conversion is (C'/(B+C')). At most (q) conversions are needed, hence
   134	
   135	\[
   136	 1-P_U^H\le q\frac{C'}{B+C'}.
   137	\]
   138	
   139	For Bd, $C'=CI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\rm Bd}(1/r)=2\sigma\,o(C^{-1})=o(C^{-1})$, while the conversion rate $B\to2r^2/(r+1)>0$. For dB, $C'=(2/r)J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1})$, while $B=CrJ_P=r\sigma>0$. Thus $qC'/B=o(q/C)=o(\eta)$, uniformly on a fixed fitness compact. The exact first-step identity
   140	
   141	\[
   142	 P_U^P=\frac{A}{A+D}p_{1,1}
   143	 =\frac{A}{A+D}\frac{B+C'}B P_U^H
   144	\]
   145	
   146	then gives (P_U^P\to Z_U/(1+Z_U)). No branching-survival substitution is hidden here.
   147	
   148	All rate and response denominators are positive for $r>1,\sigma>0$: $r-1>0$, $1+\sigma(r^2-1)>1$, $\sigma+2r(r-1)>\sigma$, and $1+Z_U>1$.
   149	
   150	## 3. Response functions and feasibility gap
   151	
   152	Writing the two brackets as $\mathcal B,\mathcal D$ to avoid confusion with the gate-rate labels,
   153	
   154	\[
   155	 \mathcal B(r;\sigma,\lambda)
   156	 =\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)}+\frac{\lambda}{r-1},
   157	\]
   158	
   159	\[
   160	 \mathcal D(r;\sigma,\lambda)
   161	 =\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}-\lambda.
   162	\]
   163	
   164	The independent expansion gives the same two pair simplifications as PDF equations (43)--(44):
   165	
   166	\[
   167	 2\left(\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right)
   168	 =\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},
   169	\]
   170	
   171	\[
   172	 2\left(\frac{[1/2]Z_D/(1+Z_D)}p-1\right)
   173	 =\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
   174	\]
   175	
   176	Define
   177	
   178	\[
   179	 L=\frac{2(1-\sigma)(r-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\qquad
   180	 U=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
   181	\]
   182	
   183	Then, exactly,
   184	
   185	\[
   186	 \mathcal B=\frac{\lambda-L}{r-1},\qquad
   187	 \mathcal D=U-\lambda.
   188	\]
   189	
   190	Consequently simultaneous positivity is equivalent to (L<\lambda<U). Direct common-denominator expansion gives the useful stronger identity
   191	
   192	\[
   193	 U-L=
   194	 \frac{-2rF_r(\sigma)}
   195	 {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}\{\sigma+2r(r-1)\}},
   196	\]
   197	
   198	where
   199	
   200	\[
   201	 F_r(\sigma)=(r-1)\sigma^2
   202	 +(r^3-4r^2+3r+1)\sigma+r(2r-3).
   203	\]
   204	
   205	The denominator is strictly positive, so (L<U\iff F_r(\sigma)<0), exactly as claimed at `main.tex:1300-1317`.
   206	
   207	## 4. Minimizer, sextic isolation, tangency, and monotonicity
   208	
   209	Because (r-1>0), (F_r) is a strictly convex quadratic in σ. Completing the square gives
   210	
   211	\[
   212	 \sigma(r)=\frac{-r^3+4r^2-3r-1}{2(r-1)},
   213	\qquad
   214	 \min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)=-\frac{P(r)}{4(r-1)},
   215	\]
   216	
   217	with
   218	
   219	\[
   220	 P(r)=r^6-8r^5+22r^4-30r^3+21r^2-6r+1.
   221	\]
   222	
   223	Equivalently, the discriminant of (F_r) as a quadratic in σ is exactly (P(r)). These identities independently reproduce `main.tex:1318-1322`.
   224	
   225	An independent Sturm sequence has sign-variation counts
   226	
   227	\[
   228	 V(1)=4,\qquad V(3/2)=4,\qquad V(151/100)=3.
   229	\]
   230	
   231	Thus there is no root in ((1,3/2)) and exactly one in ((3/2,151/100)). Exact endpoint evaluation gives
   232	
   233	\[
   234	 P(3/2)=1/64>0,
   235	\qquad
   236	 P(151/100)=-39866792399/10^{12}<0.
   237	\]
   238	
   239	The isolated root and resulting parameters are
   240	
   241	\[
   242	 R_{\rm hyb}=1.5028569127905696267\ldots,
   243	\quad \sigma_*=0.13067728228704837686\ldots,
   244	\quad \lambda_*=0.75080648303188049230\ldots.
   245	\]
   246	
   247	The sign premises used in Lemma 14 are exact, not merely decimal. For (3/2<r<151/100), writing (g(r)=-r^3+4r^2-3r-1=r^2(4-r)-3r-1), crude rational interval bounds already give
   248	
   249	\[
   250	 0<29/400<g(r)<801/4000,
   251	\]
   252	
   253	and (2(r-1)\ge1), so (0<\sigma(r)<801/4000<1). Moreover
   254	
   255	\[
   256	 \sigma(r)(r-1)^2
   257	 <\frac{801}{4000}\left(\frac{51}{100}\right)^2<1.
   258	\]
   259	
   260	At $r=R_{\rm hyb}$, $P(r)=0$ makes the quadratic minimum zero, hence $F_r(\sigma_*)=0$ and $\partial_\sigma F_r(\sigma_*)=0$: this is the asserted double tangency. Since $\lambda_*=L(R_{\rm hyb},\sigma_*)$, the gap identity also gives $U(R_{\rm hyb},\sigma_*)=\lambda_*$.
   261	
   262	Finally,
   263	
   264	\[
   265	 \partial_rL=
   266	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)\{\sigma(r-1)^2-1\}}
   267	 {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}^2}>0,
   268	\qquad
   269	 \partial_rU=
   270	 \frac{4r(\sigma-r)}{\{\sigma+2r(r-1)\}^2}<0.
   271	\]
   272	
   273	The exact signs above imply (L(r,\sigma_*)<\lambda_*<U(r,\sigma_*)) for every (1<r<R_{\rm hyb}), and both responses vanish at the endpoint.
   274	
   275	### Fixed-parameter optimality: scope and proof verified
   276	
   277	On ((R_{\rm hyb},151/100]), uniqueness of the root and the negative value at (151/100) give (P(r)<0). Hence
   278	
   279	\[
   280	 \min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)=-P(r)/\{4(r-1)\}>0.
   281	\]
   282	
   283	No real σ can be feasible at any such (r). Any putative response-positive interval beginning at one and extending beyond (R_{\rm hyb}) contains such points immediately to its right, so Proposition 15 follows.
   284	
   285	The manuscript scopes this result correctly: it is optimality only among **fixed positive** ((\sigma,\lambda)) in the displayed first-order dilute pair--pendant response model. It is not an upper bound on (R_{\rm sim}), and pp. 18--19 / `main.tex:1449-1458` explicitly exclude singular or size-dependent parameters, other scales, second-order boundary gains, nonseparated dynamics, other modules, and non-dilute interactions. I found no optimization overclaim.
   286	
   287	## 5. Rational-edge specialization
   288	
   289	For $\sigma=19/137$, $\lambda=20/27$, direct exact substitution at $r=3/2$ gives
   290	
   291	\[
   292	 \mathcal B=\frac{232}{17361}>0,
   293	 \qquad
   294	 \mathcal D=\frac{65}{12123}>0,
   295	\]
   296	
   297	as stated in Corollary 16. The full dB response factors as
   298	
   299	\[
   300	 \mathcal D(r)=
   301	 -\frac{2(6439r^2-10138r+703)}
   302	 {27(274r^2-274r+19)}.
   303	\]
   304	
   305	Its denominator is positive for (r>1), and the unique root in ((1,2)) is
   306	
   307	\[
   308	 R_{\mathbb Q}=\frac{5069+12\sqrt{147001}}{6439}
   309	 =1.5017681522336868846\ldots>3/2.
   310	\]
   311	
   312	The Bd response is
   313	
   314	\[
   315	 \mathcal B(r)=
   316	 \frac{4(95r^2-1593r+2183)}
   317	 {27(r-1)(19r^2+118)}.
   318	\]
   319	
   320	It is still positive at (R_{\mathbb Q}). One entirely rational sign check is: the dB quadratic is already positive at (751/500), so (R_{\mathbb Q}<751/500); the Bd numerator is decreasing on ((1,2)), and at (751/500) its numerator, over the common denominator (250000), is (1{,}158{,}595>0). Thus it is positive at the smaller (R_{\mathbb Q}). The monotonicity signs for (L,U) then prove both responses positive throughout ((1,R_{\mathbb Q})).
   321	
   322	Every specialized graph weight is rational: unit core/pendant weights; (W_t=C_t/\sigma=137C_t/19); (m_t=\lfloor(20/27)t\rfloor); and the least-dyadic weak cut (2^{-e_t}). The phrase "rational-edge family" is therefore exact.
   323	
   324	## 6. Effective diagonal and exact quantifier order
   325	
   326	### Denominator and decidability check
   327	
   328	For every positive weak cut, the graph is finite and connected. Every nonabsorbing configuration of either update chain is transient, so its transient matrix (Q(r)) has spectral radius below one. Therefore (I-Q(r)) is a nonsingular (M)-matrix; in particular its determinant and all event denominators are positive. The Bd denominators (F(S)) and (d_u) are positive, and each dB parent-choice denominator is a positive weighted sum because every vertex has positive degree. The same argument applies after normalizing the finite macro generator to a transient discrete chain.
   329	
   330	For fixed (t,e), all weights lie in (\mathbb K=\mathbb Q(R_{\rm hyb})) and all first-step entries are in (\mathbb K(r)). Cramer's rule thus makes actual fixation, separated fixation, and the finite complete-graph baseline rational functions over (\mathbb K). Clearing the verified positive denominators turns each absolute-value condition in Lemma 2 into two polynomial inequalities on the algebraic interval (I_t). Exact real-algebraic decision is therefore valid in principle.
   331	
   332	Existence does not require a uniform rate in $t$: for each fixed $t$, $I_t$ is a compact subset of $(1,\infty)$, and Proposition 5 gives uniform convergence as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ on that one interval. Hence at least one dyadic exponent is admissible, and testing positive integers terminates at the least $e_t$. This verifies the logical content of Lemma 2; it does not supply a useful complexity or size bound, and none is claimed.
   333	
   334	### Diagonal transfer
   335	
   336	The exact quantifier order is
   337	
   338	\[
   339	 \exists\{G_t\}_{t\ge2}\ 
   340	 \forall r\in(1,R_{\rm hyb})\ 
   341	 \exists t_0(r)\ 
   342	 \forall t\ge t_0(r):
   343	 \quad \rho_{\rm Bd}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\rm Bd}(K_{n_t},r),\quad
   344	 \rho_{\rm dB}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\rm dB}(K_{n_t},r).
   345	\]
   346	
   347	The sequence is chosen first: (C_t=t^4,q_t=t,m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor,W_t=C_t/\sigma_*), and (e_t) is selected by a supremum over the whole expanding interval (I_t=[1+1/t,R_{\rm hyb}-1/t]), not at a later chosen fitness.
   348	
   349	For every fixed interior (r), eventually (r\in I_t). Proposition 13 and Lemma 14 give
   350	
   351	\[
   352	 \frac{n_t}{q_t}
   353	 \left(\frac{\rho^0_{U,t}(r)}{\rho_U(K_{n_t},r)}-1\right)
   354	 \longrightarrow \mathcal R_U(r)>0,
   355	\]
   356	
   357	because ((n_t/q_t)(q_t/C_t)=n_t/C_t\to1). Lemma 2 changes this scaled gain by at most (1/t). The actual connected-graph gain is therefore positive eventually for each rule. This is exactly Definition 1's pointwise-in-fitness eventual quantifier, not a stronger unclaimed uniform (t_0) over the open interval.
   358	
   359	## Findings and unresolved dependencies
   360	
   361	### RESP-01 — Verified, no adverse finding — algebra/optimization
   362	
   363	All response, gap, derivative, minimizer, sextic, endpoint, rational-margin, and threshold identities pass independent exact calculation. The independent Sturm variation counts reproduce the stated root isolation.
   364	
   365	### RESP-02 — Verified, no adverse finding — diagonal/quantifiers
   366	
   367	The least-dyadic construction is fitness-independent, its existence follows from fixed-(t) compact uniformity, and the scaled-error inequality has exactly the strength needed for the pointwise eventual theorem.
   368	
   369	### RESP-DEP-01 — Cross-audit dependency, not a defect in this slice — stochastic asymptotics
   370	
   371	The response conclusion is conditional on Proposition 6's (o(q/C)), (o(C^{-1})), and compact-uniform estimates and Proposition 5's weak-cut trace. Their proofs contain expected-value, logarithmic-time, stopping, Green-function, and renewal claims (`main.tex:499-1140`). The symbolic response calculations do not validate those claims. No inconsistency in their use was found: every imported error is at or below the scale required in Proposition 13. These dependencies are assigned to the separate stochastic-mathematics audit.
   372	
   373	## Reproducibility note
   374	
   375	`independent_checks/response_math/independent_response_audit.py` reconstructs the complete-graph odds, gate ratios, full trace derivatives, feasibility gap, quadratic minimum, sextic discriminant, Sturm counts, tangency signs, and rational-edge specialization. Its final run passed using exact SymPy arithmetic. The script is evidentiary support; the derivations above state the mathematical reasons and do not treat program output as proof by itself.
   376	
   377	**Final completion estimate:** **100% of assigned slice.** No unresolved response-algebra or quantifier gap remains; only the explicitly separated stochastic premises remain outside this report.
     1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
     2	"""Independent exact audit of the response/optimization calculations.
     3	
     4	This file deliberately re-derives the displayed formulas from the component
     5	limits rather than importing or calling the authors' certificate programs.
     6	"""
     7	
     8	from __future__ import annotations
     9	
    10	import sympy as sp
    11	
    12	
    13	r, s, lam, eta = sp.symbols("r s lam eta", positive=True)
    14	p = (r - 1) / r
    15	
    16	
    17	def variation_count(sequence: list[sp.Expr], x: sp.Rational) -> int:
    18	    signs: list[int] = []
    19	    for term in sequence:
    20	        value = sp.sign(term.subs(r, x))
    21	        if value == 0:
    22	            continue
    23	        assert value in (-1, 1)
    24	        signs.append(int(value))
    25	    return sum(a != b for a, b in zip(signs, signs[1:]))
    26	
    27	
    28	def main() -> None:
    29	    # Complete-graph baselines from birth/death count-chain odds.  The dB
    30	    # products telescope even though the one-step odds are not constant.
    31	    for n_value in range(2, 13):
    32	        for r_value in (sp.Rational(3, 2), sp.Rational(2), sp.Rational(7, 3)):
    33	            bd_products = [sp.Integer(1)]
    34	            db_products = [sp.Integer(1)]
    35	            db_product = sp.Integer(1)
    36	            for k in range(1, n_value):
    37	                bd_products.append(r_value ** (-k))
    38	                db_odds = (
    39	                    n_value - 1 + (r_value - 1) * k
    40	                ) / (
    41	                    r_value
    42	                    * (n_value - 1 + (r_value - 1) * (k - 1))
    43	                )
    44	                db_product *= db_odds
    45	                expected_product = r_value ** (-k) * (
    46	                    n_value - 1 + (r_value - 1) * k
    47	                ) / (n_value - 1)
    48	                assert sp.factor(db_product - expected_product) == 0
    49	                db_products.append(db_product)
    50	            rho_bd_chain = sp.cancel(1 / sum(bd_products))
    51	            rho_db_chain = sp.cancel(1 / sum(db_products))
    52	            rho_bd_formula = (1 - 1 / r_value) / (1 - r_value ** (-n_value))
    53	            rho_db_formula = (
    54	                sp.Rational(n_value - 1, n_value)
    55	                * (1 - 1 / r_value)
    56	                / (1 - r_value ** (-(n_value - 1)))
    57	            )
    58	            assert sp.factor(rho_bd_chain - rho_bd_formula) == 0
    59	            assert sp.factor(rho_db_chain - rho_db_formula) == 0
    60	
    61	    # Gate ratios from the four limiting component rates.  These are the
    62	    # leading limits of the manuscript's A and D columns.
    63	    A_bd = 2 * s * (r - 1)
    64	    D_bd = 2 / (r + 1)
    65	    A_db = 2 * (r - 1)
    66	    D_db = s / r
    67	    Z_bd = sp.cancel(A_bd / D_bd)
    68	    Z_db = sp.cancel(A_db / D_db)
    69	    assert sp.factor(Z_bd - s * (r**2 - 1)) == 0
    70	    assert sp.factor(Z_db - 2 * r * (r - 1) / s) == 0
    71	
    72	    pair_bd = r / (r + 1) * Z_bd / (1 + Z_bd)
    73	    pair_db = sp.Rational(1, 2) * Z_db / (1 + Z_db)
    74	
    75	    # Reconstruct the full normalized traces at first order.  C is scaled to
    76	    # one, m/C=lam*eta, q/C=eta.  The center numerators are p+lam*eta for Bd
    77	    # and p for dB; the common vertex-count denominator is 1+(lam+2)*eta.
    78	    trace_bd_over_p = (p + lam * eta + 2 * eta * pair_bd) / (
    79	        p * (1 + (lam + 2) * eta)
    80	    )
    81	    trace_db_over_p = (p + 2 * eta * pair_db) / (
    82	        p * (1 + (lam + 2) * eta)
    83	    )
    84	    B_from_trace = sp.factor(sp.diff(trace_bd_over_p, eta).subs(eta, 0))
    85	    D_from_trace = sp.factor(sp.diff(trace_db_over_p, eta).subs(eta, 0))
    86	
    87	    B = 2 * (s - 1) / (1 + s * (r**2 - 1)) + lam / (r - 1)
    88	    D = 2 * (r * (2 - r) - s) / (s + 2 * r * (r - 1)) - lam
    89	    assert sp.factor(B_from_trace - B) == 0
    90	    assert sp.factor(D_from_trace - D) == 0
    91	
    92	    pair_response_bd = sp.factor(2 * (pair_bd / p - 1))
    93	    pair_response_db = sp.factor(2 * (pair_db / p - 1))
    94	    assert sp.factor(
    95	        pair_response_bd - 2 * (s - 1) / (1 + s * (r**2 - 1))
    96	    ) == 0
    97	    assert sp.factor(
    98	        pair_response_db
    99	        - 2 * (r * (2 - r) - s) / (s + 2 * r * (r - 1))
   100	    ) == 0
   101	    # The remaining terms are exactly the pendant displacements:
   102	    # lam*(1/p-1)=lam/(r-1) for Bd, and -lam for dB.
   103	    assert sp.factor(lam * (1 / p - 1) - lam / (r - 1)) == 0
   104	
   105	    L = 2 * (1 - s) * (r - 1) / (1 + s * (r**2 - 1))
   106	    U = 2 * (r * (2 - r) - s) / (s + 2 * r * (r - 1))
   107	    assert sp.factor(B - (lam - L) / (r - 1)) == 0
   108	    assert sp.factor(D - (U - lam)) == 0
   109	
   110	    F = (r - 1) * s**2 + (r**3 - 4 * r**2 + 3 * r + 1) * s + r * (
   111	        2 * r - 3
   112	    )
   113	    gap_num, gap_den = sp.cancel(U - L).as_numer_denom()
   114	    assert sp.factor(gap_num + 2 * r * F) == 0
   115	    assert sp.factor(
   116	        gap_den
   117	        - (1 + s * (r**2 - 1)) * (s + 2 * r * (r - 1))
   118	    ) == 0
   119	
   120	    sigma_min = sp.factor(-sp.diff(F, s).subs(s, 0) / (2 * (r - 1)))
   121	    expected_sigma = (-r**3 + 4 * r**2 - 3 * r - 1) / (2 * (r - 1))
   122	    assert sp.factor(sigma_min - expected_sigma) == 0
   123	
   124	    P = r**6 - 8 * r**5 + 22 * r**4 - 30 * r**3 + 21 * r**2 - 6 * r + 1
   125	    min_F = sp.factor(F.subs(s, sigma_min))
   126	    assert sp.factor(min_F + P / (4 * (r - 1))) == 0
   127	    assert sp.factor(sp.discriminant(F, s) - P) == 0
   128	
   129	    dL = sp.factor(sp.diff(L, r))
   130	    dU = sp.factor(sp.diff(U, r))
   131	    expected_dL = 2 * (s - 1) * (s * (r - 1) ** 2 - 1) / (
   132	        1 + s * (r**2 - 1)
   133	    ) ** 2
   134	    expected_dU = 4 * r * (s - r) / (s + 2 * r * (r - 1)) ** 2
   135	    assert sp.factor(dL - expected_dL) == 0
   136	    assert sp.factor(dU - expected_dU) == 0
   137	
   138	    # Independent Sturm variation counts at exact rational endpoints.
   139	    sturm = sp.sturm(P, r)
   140	    V1 = variation_count(sturm, sp.Rational(1))
   141	    V15 = variation_count(sturm, sp.Rational(3, 2))
   142	    V151 = variation_count(sturm, sp.Rational(151, 100))
   143	    assert V1 - V15 == 0
   144	    assert V15 - V151 == 1
   145	    assert P.subs(r, sp.Rational(3, 2)) == sp.Rational(1, 64)
   146	    assert P.subs(r, sp.Rational(151, 100)) == -sp.Rational(
   147	        39866792399, 10**12
   148	    )
   149	
   150	    isolating_intervals = sp.intervals(P, eps=sp.Rational(1, 10**16))
   151	    desired = [
   152	        interval
   153	        for interval, multiplicity in isolating_intervals
   154	        if multiplicity == 1
   155	        and interval[0] > sp.Rational(3, 2)
   156	        and interval[1] < sp.Rational(151, 100)
   157	    ]
   158	    assert len(desired) == 1
   159	    R = sp.CRootOf(sp.Poly(P, r), 0)
   160	    assert sp.Rational(3, 2) < R < sp.Rational(151, 100)
   161	    sigma_star = sigma_min.subs(r, R)
   162	    lambda_star = L.subs({r: R, s: sigma_star})
   163	    assert sigma_star > 0 and sigma_star < 1 and lambda_star > 0
   164	    # Tangency: F and its sigma derivative vanish at the algebraic point.
   165	    assert sp.simplify(F.subs({r: R, s: sigma_star})) == 0
   166	    assert sp.simplify(sp.diff(F, s).subs({r: R, s: sigma_star})) == 0
   167	
   168	    # Rational-edge specialization.
   169	    sr = sp.Rational(19, 137)
   170	    lr = sp.Rational(20, 27)
   171	    Bq = sp.factor(B.subs({s: sr, lam: lr}))
   172	    Dq = sp.factor(D.subs({s: sr, lam: lr}))
   173	    assert Bq.subs(r, sp.Rational(3, 2)) == sp.Rational(232, 17361)
   174	    assert Dq.subs(r, sp.Rational(3, 2)) == sp.Rational(65, 12123)
   175	    Q = 6439 * r**2 - 10138 * r + 703
   176	    Rq = (sp.Integer(5069) + 12 * sp.sqrt(147001)) / 6439
   177	    assert sp.simplify(Q.subs(r, Rq)) == 0
   178	    assert sp.factor(
   179	        Dq + 2 * Q / (27 * (274 * r**2 - 274 * r + 19))
   180	    ) == 0
   181	    assert sp.Rational(3, 2) < Rq < sp.Rational(151, 100)
   182	    assert sp.simplify(Bq.subs(r, Rq)) > 0
   183	    assert sp.simplify(F.subs({r: Rq, s: sr})) < 0
   184	
   185	    print("gate ratios:", Z_bd, Z_db)
   186	    print("complete-graph count-chain baselines: exact checks n=2,...,12 PASS")
   187	    print("response functions:", B, D)
   188	    print("gap numerator/denominator:", sp.factor(gap_num), sp.factor(gap_den))
   189	    print("Sturm variations at 1, 3/2, 151/100:", V1, V15, V151)
   190	    print("all real isolating intervals for P:", isolating_intervals)
   191	    print("R_hyb, sigma_*, lambda_*:", sp.N(R, 20), sp.N(sigma_star, 20), sp.N(lambda_star, 20))
   192	    print("rational margins at 3/2:", Bq.subs(r, sp.Rational(3, 2)), Dq.subs(r, sp.Rational(3, 2)))
   193	    print("R_Q:", sp.N(Rq, 20))
   194	    print("Bd response at R_Q (exact):", sp.radsimp(Bq.subs(r, Rq)))
   195	    print("Bd response at R_Q (decimal):", sp.N(Bq.subs(r, Rq), 20))
   196	    print("F_{R_Q}(19/137) (exact):", sp.radsimp(F.subs({r: Rq, s: sr})))
   197	    print("PASS independent response/optimization audit")
   198	
   199	
   200	if __name__ == "__main__":
   201	    main()
