FILE:work/THEOREM_LEDGER.md
# Theorem and quantifier ledger

Source: `delivered_copy/source_and_certificates/universal_simultaneous_amplification/phase4_landmark_closure/paper_hybrid_threshold/main.tex` (1,615 lines; PDF 20 pages).

All entries below are claims under review, not premises accepted from the author.

## Global model and order of limits

- Graphs are finite, connected, loopless, undirected, and have symmetric nonnegative weights with positive weighted degree at every vertex (LaTeX 143–147; PDF p. 2).
- Mutant fitness is fixed at `r>1`; residents have fitness one. Fixation is averaged over a uniformly random single-mutant start (LaTeX 148–176; PDF pp. 2–3).
- The complete-graph comparators have the same order `n=|V(G)|` as the candidate graph (LaTeX 179–185; PDF p. 3).
- Central quantifier order (LaTeX 102–110, 191–199, 324–336, 1348–1356):
  `exists one family {G_t}; for every fixed r in (1,R); there exists t_0(r); for every t>=t_0(r)` both strict inequalities hold. The graph and every weight may depend on `t`, never on the subsequently fixed `r`.
- Population scale: `C=t^4`, `q=t`, `m=floor(lambda_* t)`, `n=C+m+2q`, so `eta=q/C=t^-3=Theta(n^-3/4)` and `m/q -> lambda_*` (LaTeX 222–225, 1251–1270, 1460–1464).
- Limit order used in the proof: first obtain the exact finite-module trace as `epsilon -> 0` for fixed `(C,m,q)`; obtain compact-uniform population asymptotics as `t -> infinity`; then use an exact least-dyadic diagonal `epsilon_t` to transfer the separated result to one connected family (LaTeX 276–321, 368–440, 1348–1356).

## Results ledger

| Result | Exact hypotheses and claimed conclusion | Uniformity / scale / equality conditions | Claimed dependencies | Provisional audit state |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Definition 1: `R_sim` | Supremum of `R>1` admitting one fitness-independent growing family strictly beating both same-order complete-graph baselines for every fixed `r in (1,R)` eventually (191–199; p. 3). | Open fitness interval; pointwise eventual threshold may depend on `r`; strict inequalities; `|V(G_t)| -> infinity`. | Model and baselines. | Quantifier order internally consistent. |
| Lemma 2: effective dyadic diagonal | For each `t`, the least positive `e_t` satisfying the two compact supremum inequalities exists and is exactly computable; `epsilon_t=2^-e_t` is fixed before fitness is quantified (276–322; p. 4). | Uniform on `I_t=[1+1/t,R_hyb-1/t]`; scaled error at most `1/t` after multiplication by `n_t/q_t`; empty intervals get `e_t=1`. | Finite absorbing systems over `Q(R_hyb)(r)`; positive event denominators; nonsingular M-matrices; Proposition 5 compact-uniform convergence; real-algebraic decision. | Algebraic/quantifier mechanism re-derived; no finite complexity bound is claimed or needed. |
| Theorem 3: main theorem | The constructed family strictly amplifies every fixed `1<r<R_hyb` under Bd and dB for all sufficiently large `t`; hence `R_sim>=R_hyb>3/2` (324–336; p. 5). | Pointwise in fixed `r`; endpoint not included; `t_0` may depend on `r`; normalized gain is only order `q/C`. | Lemma 2, Proposition 13, Lemma 14, final diagonal transfer (1348–1356). | Pending final reconciliation; no illicit limit exchange found. |
| Lemma 4: strong orbit lumping | For every positive weak-edge weight and either rule, aggregation by `(h,i,u,v,l)` is strongly lumpable for arbitrary finite construction sizes (341–366; p. 5). | Exact, all fitness values allowed by model and every positive cut; not a nine-vertex-only claim. | Wreath-product automorphism action preserves weights, degrees, types, fitness, and ordered events. | General group-action proof valid; independent different-parameter orbit enumeration passed. |
| Proposition 5: finite weak-cut trace | Gives the exact separated initialization formula and compact-uniform convergence of the connected fixation probability as `epsilon -> 0` (368–441; pp. 5–6). | Fixed finite `(C,m,q)`; uniform on each compact subset of `(0,infinity)`; global absorbing macro boundaries retained. | Fast mixed states transient at zero cut; row-oriented scaled Schur complement `A+B(I-Q_0)^-1 C_0`; invertible macro interior. | Orientation, state partition, and compact inverse argument re-derived consistently. |
| Proposition 6: center module | Ordinary-core fixation is `p+o(q/C)` under both rules; Bd mutant leaf fixes with `1-o(1)`, dB leaf with `O(C^-1)`; gives center averages and reciprocal `o(C^-1)` portal bounds (443–466; pp. 6–7). | Uniform on each fixed compact fitness subset of `(1,infinity)` along `C=t^4,q=t,m~t`; reciprocal fitness is `1/r`. | Lemmas 7–11. | All load-bearing probability scales under adversarial review; one local time-display error occurs inside Lemma 9 (below). |
| Lemma 7: early establishment | From one ordinary mutant, hub/leaf coordinates change before `{0,K}` with `O(K^2/C)` probability, and hitting `K` first has probability `1-1/r+O(K^2/C)+O(r^-K)` (499–543; pp. 7–8). | `K=ceil(A_0 log C)`; compact-uniform in `r` and both rules; fixed `A_0`. | Exact embedded up/down odds; positive adapted drift; product-odds formula. | Rates and error accumulation independently re-derived. |
| Lemma 8: completion and confinement | Establishment reaches density `1-delta` except `O(r_0^-K)+e^-gamma C`; deficit stays below `2delta c` over polynomial horizons with exponentially small failure and returns to a fixed boundary layer with exponential tail (544–660; pp. 8–9). | Uniform in hidden hub/leaf coordinates, compact fitness, any fixed horizon exponent `M`; nested stopping boundaries explicit. | Conditional-intensity comparison, exponential supermartingale, compensator count, generator drift. | No Markov assumption on hidden-coordinate marginal; checks consistent. |
| Lemma 9: cleanup and pendant starts | From high core density, fixation occurs in polynomial displayed time except `O(C^-B0)` plus exponential escape; Bd leaf fixation `1-o(1)`, dB leaf fixation `O(C^-1)` (662–932; pp. 9–13). | Any fixed `B_0`; compact-uniform; Bd uses conditional block success rather than independence; dB uses `T=beta_0(B_0) log C`. | Two-state renewal monotonicity; pendant trace; nested Bd block recursion; dB pure-death/core contraction; graphical coupling. | **Local false display:** eq. (26), lines 749–755, states unconditional `E tau_{l=m}=O(Cm)`, but `tau=∞` on the positive-probability extinction event. Later use needs a stopped time at core-strip exit and then activation; likely repairable without changing the theorem. |
| Lemma 10: reciprocal killed Green | Subcritical ordinary excursion has exponentially small macro escape, bounded particle-time, exponential tail, hence hub-change probability `O_L(C^-1)` with tail (934–995; p. 13). | Fixed `L`; uniform reciprocal compact and both rules; stop at `0` or `ceil(delta c)`. | Dominated nearest-neighbor walk; Dynkin particle-time; hazard integration. | Re-derived consistently. |
| Lemma 11: reciprocal renewal | `u_core^Bd(1/r)=o(C^-1)` and `J_H(1/r)=o(C^-1)` (997–1119; pp. 14–15). | Compact-uniform; nested limit order: fixed truncations as `C->infinity`, then `L_2`, `L_1`, and time horizon in the displayed order. | Lemma 10; Bd hub-loss decomposition; dB immigration-death domination and finite-horizon maximum; renewal refinement. | Limit order and `o`, not merely `O`, were independently checked. |
| Proposition 12: gates and sweep | `P_U^H=1-o(q/C)` and pair-start macro fixation tends to `Z/(1+Z)` with `Z_B=sigma(r^2-1)`, `Z_D=2r(r-1)/sigma` (1166–1247; pp. 16–17). | Fixed `r>1`, compact-uniform; adverse center reversals retained; complete sweep error smaller than gain scale. | Four exact introduction rates; Proposition 6 portal bounds; exact two-coordinate macro chain. | Rate directions and ratios independently derived; union bound is conservative and sufficient. |
| Proposition 13: two responses | Separated normalized Bd/dB corrections equal `1+eta B+o(eta)` and `1+eta D+o(eta)` with the displayed rational responses (1251–1290; p. 17). | Fixed positive `(sigma,lambda)`; compact-uniform in fitness; complete-graph errors are `o(eta)`. | Proposition 5 trace, Proposition 6 center terms, Proposition 12 gates, exact pair values. | Independent expansion and exact rational samples pass. |
| Lemma 14: exact tangency | At `(sigma_*,lambda_*)`, both first-order corrections are strictly positive on `(1,R_hyb)` and zero together at `R_hyb` (1292–1346; pp. 17–18). | Strict interior signs; endpoint equality only; all denominators positive. | Feasibility interval `L<lambda<U`; convex quadratic `F_r`; sextic `P`; exact Sturm isolation; monotonicity. | Independent standard-library Sturm sequence gives root counts `0` then `1`; formulas and signs pass. |
| Proposition 15: fixed-response optimality | `R_hyb` is the largest threshold for an interval beginning at one among fixed positive parameters in this first-order response model (1359–1382; p. 18). | Does not cover singular/size-dependent parameters, other density scales/modules, second order, or unrestricted graph families. | Quadratic minimum and sextic sign immediately to the right of `R_hyb`; Lemma 14 supplies attainability below. | Scope is accurately limited; local infeasibility beyond endpoint suffices to rule out any longer interval. |
| Corollary 16: rational family | `sigma=19/137`, `lambda=20/27` yields exact positive margins at `r=3/2` and threshold `(5069+12 sqrt(147001))/6439 >3/2`; all weights rational (1384–1416; pp. 18–19). | Open interval ending at the dB response root; Bd response remains positive there; least dyadic cut repeated on expanding algebraic intervals. | Proposition 13 response algebra; monotonicity; rational/algebraic exact decision. | Exact fractions, discriminant, root location, and positivity independently pass. |

## Certificate boundary

- The nine-vertex program exhausts 512 labelled configurations and 108 orbit fibres at one rational parameter point. It checks the orientation and declared transition aggregation at that point; it cannot prove arbitrary-size lumpability, the weak-cut limit, or any population asymptotic.
- The symbolic programs check algebraic identities, exact endpoint values, root counts, and marker integration. They do not certify stochastic estimates or any infinite quantifier.
- `verify_paper_claims.py` is a marker/integration regression audit, not a proof checker.
- The analytic proof and software claim the same finite certificate boundary. A successful replay is supporting reproducibility evidence only.
FILE:independent_checks/package_code_audit.md
# Independent package and software source audit

**Checkpoint:** 2026-08-22 (America/Los_Angeles)  
**Auditor:** independent code-audit subagent  
**Package audited:** `delivered_copy/`  
**Best-guess completion:** **70% toward the full software/reproducibility audit; 100% of the pre-execution source-inspection phase.** Runtime certificate replay and deterministic PDF/archive rebuild are still unperformed.  
**Execution boundary:** I did **not** execute any delivered shell script or Python program, including `verify_referee_package.py` and `run_all_referee_checks.sh`. I used only independently written read-only checks and system inspection tools. I did not modify `delivered_copy`, use the network, contact anyone, upload, commit, or push.

## Bottom line

The delivered bytes are internally complete and consistent with both bundled manifests: 29 outer payload files, 19 sorted regular archive members (18 payloads plus the internal manifest), byte-identical archive/extracted payloads, and byte-identical convenience/source PDFs. The claimed digests are present and match the delivered bytes.

That establishes **internal package consistency**, not external provenance. The package contains neither Git objects nor a signature binding its bytes to the claimed commit/tag. In addition, the mathematical certifiers and the bootstrap's version checks rely entirely on Python `assert`; an inherited `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=1` strips those checks while the scripts and top-level runner can still print `PASS`. This is the most important software finding.

The canonical replay is not presently runnable under the audit's no-network rule: Python 3.14.6 and the document tools are present, but SymPy and mpmath are absent, no wheels are vendored, and the bootstrap explicitly disables pip's cache and installs from a configured package index.

## Findings

### CODE-01 — High — verifier soundness — optimization can erase every mathematical check

All four mathematical programs express their checks as bare `assert` statements:

- `certificates/verify_leading_algebra.py:15-66` (11 asserts),
- `certificates/verify_hybrid_lumping.py:159` (the sole assert, repeated in the enumeration loop),
- `certificates/verify_hybrid_coefficients.py:16-106` (21 asserts), and
- `verify_paper_claims.py:32-154` (25 asserts).

The bootstrap's Python and dependency version checks are also assertions (`bootstrap_replay.sh:9-12` and `23-29`). None of `bootstrap_replay.sh`, `replay.sh`, or `run_all_referee_checks.sh` rejects optimized Python or clears `PYTHONOPTIMIZE` (`bootstrap_replay.sh:1-32`; `replay.sh:1-21`; `run_all_referee_checks.sh:1-76`). Each certificate prints a success message after the assertion block, and `run_all_referee_checks.sh:76` prints its aggregate `PASS` after the nested programs return zero.

Independent runtime demonstration with the installed interpreter showed that `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=1 python3 -c 'assert False; print(...)'` exits zero and prints the trailing message. Therefore the complete certificate suite can become a false-pass suite in a supported-looking environment. The variable is unset in the current audit environment, so this is a latent design flaw rather than evidence that the delivered outputs were produced under optimization.

**Required correction:** replace verification-critical assertions with explicit checks that raise exceptions; additionally reject `sys.flags.optimize != 0` using an explicit conditional. Running the certifiers with `-I` and a sanitized environment would reduce import/environment contamination but does not replace explicit checks.

### CODE-02 — High limitation — identity/provenance — commit and tag are asserted, not authenticated

The verifier hard-codes a commit and tag (`verify_referee_package.py:23-24`) and only checks that those strings occur in `VERSION.md` and `README_FIRST.md` (`verify_referee_package.py:149-161`). It does not reconstruct a Git tree, inspect a commit object, verify a signed tag, or compare against an independently authenticated digest. The archive metadata does not contain the claimed commit/tag (`source_and_certificates/BUNDLE_METADATA.txt:1-14`), and the delivered package contains no `.git` objects or signature.

The source does contain the tag in a GitHub URL (`main.tex:1476`) and the wrapper repeats the commit/tag (`README_FIRST.md:15-26`; `VERSION.md:8-12`), but these are mutually dependent author-supplied statements. The outer manifest is also shipped inside the same unauthenticated package and intentionally cannot hash itself.

**Exact conclusion:** offline checks establish that the delivered archive is SHA-256 `ce62bfbdb22681ba48b2a04653155b2e06f52659f140c13f5e0220db365b9250` and the PDF is SHA-256 `f68142b3d99b95f83ca6ba4688539cb9e0fdb88ed96809aef5316ed22a59888f`; they do not establish that those bytes came from commit `2302d7c6ae17fc061a985da322df6d0600b66672` or the claimed immutable tag. A trusted external record, signed tag/manifest, or included Git object proof would be needed for that stronger identity claim.

### CODE-03 — Medium — hermeticity/supply chain — the frozen handoff is not offline self-contained

`bootstrap_replay.sh:18-21` creates/uses a virtual environment and performs `pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt`. `requirements.txt:1-2` pins only `mpmath==1.3.0` and `sympy==1.14.0`; it supplies neither artifacts nor hashes. `README_FIRST.md:61-66` accurately discloses that distributions are not vendored/hash-pinned and that pip and Tectonic may access external resources, notwithstanding the description “self-contained” at `README_FIRST.md:3`.

The current environment has Python 3.14.6 but has neither SymPy nor mpmath installed, and the archive contains no wheels or source distributions. Because a fresh venv does not inherit global site packages and pip caching is disabled, the canonical bootstrap cannot complete under the present no-network constraint unless a separately configured local index is supplied. Tectonic package/resource inputs are likewise not included or individually hash-pinned; the final PDF byte comparison would detect a divergent result but does not make the build offline-hermetic.

**Consequence:** exact runtime replay is presently blocked by an unavailable dependency source, not by the delivered mathematical code. Installing from the network would add unreviewed executable supply-chain inputs and is outside this audit's authorization.

### CODE-04 — Medium — deterministic rebuild portability — archive builder ignores the pinned interpreter handle

The canonical wrapper honors `BOOTSTRAP_PYTHON` for bootstrap/replay (`run_all_referee_checks.sh:5,16,53`), but `release_bundle.sh:17-19` invokes bare `python3` for `bundle_manifest.py`. It does not use the venv interpreter, `BOOTSTRAP_PYTHON`, or the interpreter selected by `replay.sh`. Archive bytes depend on Python's `gzip`/`tarfile` implementation (`bundle_manifest.py:155-165`), so the rebuild is not actually tied to the bootstrap interpreter when `python3` resolves elsewhere.

The current `python3` is the claimed 3.14.6, so the inconsistency does not block this host. It does defeat the documented escape hatch when a reviewer must set `BOOTSTRAP_PYTHON=/path/to/python3.14.6` because their PATH's `python3` is different or absent.

**Suggested correction:** pass the selected interpreter through to `release_bundle.sh` and use it for `bundle_manifest.py`, or call the created venv's interpreter explicitly.

### CODE-05 — Low — integrity scope — extracted executable modes are not checked by the standalone verifier

`verify_referee_package.py:62-81` binds outer file paths, regular-node status, and file bytes, but not permission bits. Yet `bundle_manifest.py:136-141` derives archive executable modes from source-tree permission bits. Changing an extracted wrapper from executable to non-executable (or vice versa) does not alter its SHA-256, so `verify_referee_package.py` alone can still report success.

The full `run_all_referee_checks.sh` rebuild-and-`cmp` path would catch such a change because archive metadata would differ (`run_all_referee_checks.sh:54-58`). The delivered copy's modes do match the archive: shell scripts, `bundle_manifest.py`, and `verify_paper_claims.py` are 0755; other archive members are 0644.

## Package identity and completeness checks

Independent checks produced the following verified facts:

- Exactly 30 regular files are present in `delivered_copy`: 29 outer payloads plus `PACKAGE_MANIFEST.sha256`; no symlinks or non-regular nodes were found.
- The outer manifest's set equals the complete payload file set, and all 29 hashes match.
- The detached archive checksum has the canonical exact form and matches the archive bytes.
- The source archive has exactly 19 unique, lexicographically sorted, regular members. There are no absolute paths, `..` components, symlinks, hard links, devices, or directory members.
- All archive members have fixed `mtime=1787356800`, `uid=gid=0`, `uname=gname=root`, and expected modes. The gzip header contains the same fixed mtime, no filename, maximum-compression flag, and OS-neutral byte.
- The internal manifest covers exactly the other 18 archive members; all internal SHA-256 values match.
- `source_and_certificates/` contains exactly the archive member set and is byte-identical to the archive payload, including its manifest.
- The convenience PDF and nested source-tree PDF are byte-identical.
- Archive whitelist source inspection (`bundle_manifest.py:20-41,128-170,174-210`) agrees with the delivered 19-member archive. The builder fixes ordering, metadata, and modes and performs an internal post-write verification.

These checks independently reproduce the package verifier's intended content checks without executing it. They do not resolve CODE-02's authentication limitation.

## Complete executable/import inventory

No project-local Python module is imported by any certificate. Static AST inspection found only the following:

| Program | Role | Imports | Verification asserts |
|---|---|---|---:|
| `verify_referee_package.py` | outer/archive/extraction/prompt consistency | standard library: `hashlib`, `pathlib`, `re`, `tarfile` | 0 |
| `bundle_manifest.py` | deterministic archive construction | standard library: `argparse`, `gzip`, `hashlib`, `io`, `os`, `pathlib`, `tarfile`, `tempfile` | 0 |
| `certificates/verify_leading_algebra.py` | sextic/root/tangency derivative identities | third party: `sympy` | 11 |
| `certificates/verify_hybrid_lumping.py` | labelled finite transition enumeration | standard library: `fractions`, `itertools` | 1 (inside exhaustive loop) |
| `certificates/verify_hybrid_coefficients.py` | response coefficients, rational specialization, phase algebra | third party: `sympy` | 21 |
| `verify_paper_claims.py` | duplicate algebra plus manuscript marker regression checks | standard library: `fractions`, `pathlib`; third party: `sympy` | 25 |

Shell entry points were all read in full before any prospective execution:

- `run_all_referee_checks.sh:1-76`: outer verifier, exact tool-version gates, guarded temporary copy, bootstrap replay, rebuild, byte comparisons, final hashes.
- `bootstrap_replay.sh:1-32`: Python-version assertion, venv creation/reuse, pip installation, dependency assertions, replay.
- `replay.sh:1-21`: interpreter selection and four certificate invocations.
- `build.sh:1-22`: Tectonic build, PDF installation/info, rendered-image deletion/regeneration.
- `release_bundle.sh:1-19`: replay, build, archive generation.
- `all.sh:1-6`: replay plus build.

The TeX source has no local `\input`, `\include`, `\includegraphics`, bibliography file, shell-escape, file-read, or file-write directive. Its external build dependencies are the class/package resources declared at `main.tex:1-9`.

Third-party source inspection is necessarily incomplete: SymPy, mpmath, and the Tectonic resource bundle are not delivered and SymPy/mpmath are not installed. Standard-library/runtime source was treated as part of the pinned interpreter platform rather than a project import.

## Failure propagation and mutation/safety review

Except for CODE-01, nonzero statuses propagate coherently:

- Every shell wrapper uses `set -eu`; the sequential certificate calls in `replay.sh:18-21`, build/replay calls in `release_bundle.sh:15-19`, and all top-level comparisons in `run_all_referee_checks.sh:16-74` are fail-closed on nonzero exit.
- Required document tools and exact displayed versions are explicitly checked before mutation (`run_all_referee_checks.sh:18-39`). The string equality is intentionally strict and may reject otherwise compatible builds, but it does not create a false pass.
- The top-level runner creates an unpredictable temporary directory under an absolute `TMPDIR`, copies the source tree there, and mutates only the copy (`run_all_referee_checks.sh:9-14,41-61`). Cleanup validates the generated prefix before recursive deletion (`run_all_referee_checks.sh:42-48`). I found no path-expansion route from the delivered values to a broad deletion target.
- `build.sh:19` deletes only `page-*.png` beneath its derived `output/rendered` directory. In the canonical runner that directory is inside the disposable copy.
- `bundle_manifest.py:149-168` writes a temporary sibling and atomically replaces the requested output. A caller can explicitly choose an existing output path, so standalone use is intentionally overwriting; the canonical wrapper supplies a disposable path.
- `bootstrap_replay.sh` writes a `.venv-paper2` and may execute downloaded installation artifacts. `build.sh` may populate Tectonic's resource cache. These are the only implicit external-write/network surfaces found. No delivered Python source imports networking, subprocess, HTTP, email, messaging, cloud, or repository APIs.
- `pdftoppm` diagnostics are suppressed in `build.sh:20-22`, but its failure status still propagates under `set -e`.

## Exact claim-to-code coverage

The package's own `CLAIM_CODE_MAP.md` is substantially candid. The following is the audited boundary, with stronger wording where needed.

| Claim family | Automated content actually checked | Exact gap/limitation |
|---|---|---|
| Model, baseline, theorem quantifiers (`main.tex:141-225,324-336`) | `verify_paper_claims.py:101-157` checks selected byte/text fragments only; the finite lumping code independently implements the two update rules for one instance. | No semantic verification of definitions, graph-family quantifier order, baseline formulas, or main theorem. |
| Strong orbit lumping (`main.tex:341-366`) | `verify_hybrid_lumping.py:16-160` enumerates all 512 configurations and 108 fibres under Bd and dB, comparing labelled and declared off-fibre rates exactly over `Fraction`. | Only `C=3`, two pairs, two pendants, `r=3/2`, `sigma=19/137`, and `epsilon=1/100`. It is neither a symbolic parameter proof nor a check for theorem-scale `C,q,m` or dyadic cuts. Self-loop equality follows from row sums but is not printed separately. |
| Weak-cut Schur trace and compact-uniform convergence (`main.tex:368-441`) | A manuscript marker is required by `verify_paper_claims.py:105-138`. | No matrix construction, Schur-complement computation, perturbation bound, uniform inverse bound, or convergence certificate. Entirely analytic. |
| Effective least-dyadic diagonal (`main.tex:276-322`) | `verify_paper_claims.py:94-98` merely confirms that eight example fractions `1/2^e` are positive and have expected denominators; `101-157` checks phrases. | It does not compute any `e_t`, fixation rational function, supremum, Sturm/subresultant decision, or termination witness. Computability/existence remains solely in the manuscript. |
| Center module, establishment, confinement, cleanup, reciprocal invasion (`main.tex:443-1141`) | `verify_paper_claims.py:101-157` checks regression phrases/formulas are present. | No stopped-chain, coupling, Green-function, exponential-moment, cleanup, or uniform-error calculation is automated. These analytic steps carry the main asymptotic burden. |
| Pair gate leading rates and odds (`main.tex:1142-1247`) | `verify_hybrid_coefficients.py:28-43` encodes four already-reduced leading rates and symbolically derives the two odds corrections; `verify_paper_claims.py:20-47` duplicates response reconstruction. | The programs do not derive the finite gate table from the graph, prove its portal asymptotics, check adverse-reversal error bounds, or verify the global sweep. Correctness of the encoded leading rates is an input to the algebra check. |
| Two response functions (`main.tex:1249-1290`) | Exact symbolic simplification of pair terms, pendant terms, feasibility bounds, and rational margins (`verify_hybrid_coefficients.py:28-69`; `verify_paper_claims.py:20-98`). | No verification of the `o_r(eta)` remainders or compact uniformity; these depend on the unautomated stochastic propositions. |
| Sextic root, tangency, fixed-parameter response optimum (`main.tex:1292-1382`) | Exact polynomial/root counts, feasibility-gap identity, minimum identity, derivative identities, algebraic remainder checks, and supporting decimal diagnostics (`verify_leading_algebra.py:13-71`; `verify_hybrid_coefficients.py:71-112`; `verify_paper_claims.py:50-74`). | Expressions are independently checkable but manually encoded. Some sign conclusions are explained in comments/manuscript rather than exhaustively asserted by code. `nroots` diagnostics are numerical; the root count/remainder checks provide the exact portion. |
| Rational-edge specialization (`main.tex:1384-1416`) | Exact endpoint margins, threshold quadratic identity/interval, and positivity of the Bd response at that threshold (`verify_hybrid_coefficients.py:52-69`; `verify_paper_claims.py:77-98`). | Rationality/existence of the complete least-dyadic graph sequence is not constructed by code; the finite eight-exponent loop is not a certificate for all `t`. |

Accordingly, a successful replay would validate finite algebra and one finite transition instance. It would not validate the main theorem without an independent proof audit of Sections 4-6, especially the weak-cut uniform limit and stochastic gain-scale estimates.

## Environment/tool inventory at this checkpoint

- Host: macOS 26.5.2, build 25F84; Darwin kernel 25.5.0; arm64.
- `python3`: `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3`, Python 3.14.6 (matches claim).
- SymPy: not installed.
- mpmath: not installed.
- Tectonic: `/opt/homebrew/bin/tectonic`, `Tectonic 0.16.9` (matches claim).
- `pdfinfo`: `/opt/homebrew/bin/pdfinfo`, Poppler 26.08.0 (matches claim).
- `pdftoppm`: `/opt/homebrew/bin/pdftoppm`, Poppler 26.08.0 (matches claim).
- `PYTHONOPTIMIZE`, `PYTHONPATH`, `PYTHON`, and `BOOTSTRAP_PYTHON`: unset.
- `TMPDIR`: absolute path `/var/folders/cp/bbqcpp814bjd_6mfhk6lxf7r0000gn/T/` (the top-level normalization would remove its trailing slash).
- No `.venv-paper2` or `.venv` is delivered.

## Source-inspection readiness decision

All delivered wrappers, scripts, verifiers, Python imports, manifest/archive generation logic, requirements, TeX resource declarations, and the code-facing manuscript claims have now been inspected. There are no hidden project imports. On source safety alone, the standard-library outer verifier is ready for a controlled execution. The full canonical runner is **not ready under the current no-network constraint** because its fresh environment cannot obtain SymPy/mpmath, and executing it with network access would require separate authorization and dependency-source review.

Even if dependencies become available offline, the run should first sanitize `PYTHONOPTIMIZE`/`PYTHONPATH`, record the exact installed distribution hashes, and treat a successful replay as evidence only for the bounded claims in the coverage table. `run_all_referee_checks.sh` remains deliberately unexecuted pending follow-up.
