    35	\title{A fitness-independent family of simultaneous amplifiers beyond relative fitness $3/2$}
    36	\author{Alec Kriebel\\
    37	Independent Researcher\\
    38	\href{mailto:me@aleckriebel.com}{me@aleckriebel.com}\\
    39	\href{https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X}{ORCID: 0009-0001-9320-500X}}
    40	\date{August 22, 2026}
    41	
    42	\begin{document}
    43	\maketitle
    44	
    45	\begin{abstract}
    46	We study fixation from a uniformly random single mutant on finite connected
    47	loopless undirected weighted graphs under both Birth--death (Bd) and
    48	death--Birth (dB) Moran updating.  Let $\Rsim$ be the supremum of the
    49	fitness intervals $(1,R)$ on which one graph family, chosen independently
    50	of fitness, eventually amplifies fixation under both rules.  Let $\Rhyb$ be
    51	the unique root in $(3/2,151/100)$ of
    52	\[
    53	 R^6-8R^5+22R^4-30R^3+21R^2-6R+1.
    54	\]
    55	We construct one fitness-independent family that simultaneously amplifies
    56	every fixed $1<r<\Rhyb$ for all sufficiently large members of the sequence.
    57	Therefore
    58	$\Rsim\geq\Rhyb=1.5028569127905696\ldots>3/2$.
    59	
    60	The construction combines a large clique with dilute hub pendants and
    61	dilute, internally heavy two-vertex satellites.  An exact finite-state
    62	derivation of the weak-cut limiting trace, together with uniform establishment, cleanup, and
    63	reciprocal-invasion estimates at the scale of the eventual gain, reduces the two
    64	normalized fixation corrections to explicit response functions.  Optimizing
    65	their common positivity interval yields the displayed sextic.  The same
    66	analysis proves that $\Rhyb$ is optimal among fixed positive
    67	$(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute pair--pendant response
    68	model; a specialization with entirely rational edge weights already crosses
    69	$3/2$.  Thus $3/2$ is not the exact simultaneous-amplification threshold.
    70	The unrestricted value of $\Rsim$, and even a finite universal upper bound,
    71	remain open.
    72	\end{abstract}
    73	
    74	\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} evolutionary graph theory; Moran process;
    75	fixation probability; simultaneous amplification; Birth--death updating;
    76	death--Birth updating.
    77	
    78	\medskip
    79	\noindent\textbf{Mathematics Subject Classification (2020):}
    80	92D15, 60J10, 05C81.
    81	
    82	\section{Introduction}
    83	
    84	Evolutionary graph theory extends the finite-population Moran process by
    85	representing sites as vertices and replacement opportunities as weighted
    86	edges \citep{Moran1958,Lieberman2005,Allen2017}.  The order of the two elementary Moran events matters:
    87	stars and many other structures that amplify selection when birth precedes
    88	death suppress it when death precedes birth
    89	\citep{Hindersin2015,Allen2020}.  Random edge weights also tend to make
    90	undirected networks less amplifying, and often suppressing, which emphasizes
    91	that useful weighting is a design problem rather than a generic effect
    92	\citep{Bhaumik2024}.  Death--Birth amplification is necessarily
    93	transient in fitness on every fixed noncomplete strongly connected structure
    94	\citep{Tkadlec2020}, and transient dB amplifiers have also been sought through
    95	guided spectral edge deletion \citep{Richter2023}.  A distinct approach
    96	randomizes the update order within a single mixed Moran process
    97	\citep{Brewster2026}.  Here robustness instead means that the same graph
    98	beats the well-mixed benchmark under each pure rule separately.  These
    99	tensions make robustness to update order a natural design problem rather
   100	than an automatic consequence of ordinary amplification.
   101	
   102	A population structure is a \emph{simultaneous amplifier} at fitness $r$
   103	when it raises uniformly initialized fixation probability, relative to the
   104	complete graph of the same order, under both Bd and dB updating.  The
   105	asymptotic problem has the quantifier order
   106	\[
   107	 \exists\{G_t\}\ \forall r\in(1,R)\ \exists t_0(r)\
   108	 \forall t\geq t_0(r).
   109	\]
   110	The graphs and weights may depend on $t$, but not on $r$.
   111	
   112	A complementary fixed-graph obstruction has the opposite quantifier order.
   113	No fixed finite weighted structure is a strict dB amplifier for every
   114	$r>1$: every structure not dB-equivalent to the complete graph is suppressing
   115	above a graph-dependent threshold $R_G$, whereas the equivalent class ties
   116	the baseline \citep{Kriebel2026fixed}.  This
   117	$\forall G\,\exists R_G$ statement supplies no graph-uniform threshold and
   118	therefore does not rule out the present
   119	$\exists\{G_t\}\,\forall r<R\,\exists t_0(r)$ order; the thresholds
   120	$R_{G_t}$ may vary with $t$.
   121	
   122	\citet{Svoboda2024} constructed simultaneous amplifiers on $1<r<1.2$.
   123	A preliminary unrefereed research release established $\Rsim\geq3/2$ with a
   124	center--triangle family, while that particular family suppresses at its
   125	endpoint \citep{Kriebel2026half}.  The present manuscript supersedes that
   126	release in its lower-bound conclusion by crossing $3/2$.
   127	The numerical increment is small, but the conclusion is qualitative: the
   128	earlier endpoint is not the unrestricted phase boundary.
   129	
   130	The proof has four ingredients.  First, a finite Schur complement gives the
   131	rare-migration trace without an independence or branching approximation.
   132	Second, stopped birth--death comparisons control establishment and the
   133	entire path to fixation at the smaller $q/C$ scale of the final gain.  Third,
   134	the exact two-coordinate macro chain retains adverse reversals while deriving
   135	the pair gate.  Finally, optimizing the two resulting response functions
   136	produces an algebraic tangency and the degree-six polynomial in the abstract.
   137	This also proves fixed-parameter optimality within the displayed first-order
   138	pair--pendant response model.  No universal upper bound is inferred; the
   139	unrestricted value of $\Rsim$ remains open.
   140	
   141	\section{Model, baselines, and threshold}
   142	
   143	Let $G=(V,w)$ be finite, connected, loopless, undirected, and weighted:
   144	\[
   145	 w_{uv}=w_{vu}\geq0,\qquad w_{uu}=0,\qquad
   146	 d_u:=\sum_vw_{uv}>0.
   147	\]
   148	A state is a mutant set $S\subseteq V$.  Mutants have fitness $r>1$ and
   149	residents fitness one.  Under Bd, a parent is selected proportional to
   150	fitness and then a replacement target proportional to edge weight.  Under
   151	dB, a uniformly chosen target dies and then a parent is selected
   152	proportional to fitness times edge weight.  Thus, with
   153	$F(S)=|V|+(r-1)|S|$,
   154	\begin{align}
   155	 P_{\Bd}(S,S\cup\{v\})
   156	 &=\frac r{F(S)}\sum_{u\in S}\frac{w_{uv}}{d_u},
   157	 &&v\notin S,\\
   158	 P_{\Bd}(S,S\setminus\{v\})
   159	 &=\frac1{F(S)}\sum_{u\notin S}\frac{w_{uv}}{d_u},
   160	 &&v\in S,\\
   161	 P_{\dB}(S,S\cup\{v\})
   162	 &=\frac1{|V|}
   163	 \frac{r\sum_{u\in S}w_{uv}}
   164	 {r\sum_{u\in S}w_{uv}+\sum_{u\notin S}w_{uv}},
   165	 &&v\notin S,\\
   166	 P_{\dB}(S,S\setminus\{v\})
   167	 &=\frac1{|V|}
   168	 \frac{\sum_{u\notin S}w_{uv}}
   169	 {r\sum_{u\in S}w_{uv}+\sum_{u\notin S}w_{uv}},
   170	 &&v\in S.
   171	\end{align}
   172	The fixation function $h_U$ is the unique harmonic solution with boundary
   173	values zero at $\varnothing$ and one at $V$.  We use
   174	\[
   175	 \rho_U(G,r)=\frac1{|V|}\sum_{v\in V}h_U(\{v\}),
   176	 \qquad U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}.
   177	\]
   178	
   179	Direct solution of the one-dimensional complete-graph count chains gives
   180	\begin{align}
   181	 \rho_{\Bd}(K_n,r)
   182	 &=\frac{1-r^{-1}}{1-r^{-n}},\\
   183	 \rho_{\dB}(K_n,r)
   184	 &=\frac{n-1}{n}\frac{1-r^{-1}}{1-r^{-(n-1)}}.
   185	\end{align}
   186	Both converge, for fixed $r>1$, to
   187	\[
   188	 p(r):=1-\frac1r.
   189	\]
   190	
   191	\begin{definition}
   192	$\Rsim$ is the supremum of all $R>1$ for which one
   193	fitness-independent family $\{G_t\}$, with $|V(G_t)|\to\infty$, satisfies
   194	\[
   195	 \rho_{\Bd}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\Bd}(K_{|V(G_t)|},r),\qquad
   196	 \rho_{\dB}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\dB}(K_{|V(G_t)|},r)
   197	\]
   198	for every fixed $r\in(1,R)$ and every sufficiently large $t$.
   199	\end{definition}
   200	
   201	\section{The pair--pendant construction and main theorem}
   202	
   203	Let
   204	\[
   205	 P(R)=R^6-8R^5+22R^4-30R^3+21R^2-6R+1
   206	\]
   207	and let $\Rhyb$ be its unique root in $(3/2,151/100)$.  Define
   208	\begin{align}
   209	 \sigma_*&=
   210	 \frac{-\Rhyb^3+4\Rhyb^2-3\Rhyb-1}{2(\Rhyb-1)},\\
   211	 \lambda_*&=
   212	 \frac{2(\Rhyb-1)(1-\sigma_*)}
   213	 {1+\sigma_*(\Rhyb^2-1)}.
   214	\end{align}
   215	Exact root isolation gives
   216	\[
   217	 \Rhyb=1.5028569127905696\ldots,\quad
   218	 \sigma_*=0.1306772822870484\ldots,\quad
   219	 \lambda_*=0.7508064830318805\ldots .
   220	\]
   221	
   222	For $t\geq2$, put
   223	\[
   224	 q_t=t,\qquad m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor,\qquad C_t=t^4,
   225	\qquad n_t=C_t+m_t+2q_t.
   226	\]
   227	The graph contains:
   228	\begin{enumerate}
   229	\item a unit-weight clique on $C_t$ vertices, one designated as the hub;
   230	\item $m_t$ unit-weight leaves adjacent only to the hub;
   231	\item $q_t$ disjoint two-vertex satellites, each with internal edge weight
   232	      $W_t=C_t/\sigma_*$;
   233	\item an edge of common weight $\eps_t>0$ from every satellite vertex to
   234	      every clique vertex.
   235	\end{enumerate}
   236	There are no other edges.  In particular, there are no edges between
   237	distinct satellites.
   238	
   239	\begin{figure}[ht]
   240	\centering
   241	\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95,
   242	 core/.style={circle,draw,fill=blue!12,minimum size=5mm},
   243	 pair/.style={circle,draw,fill=orange!18,minimum size=5mm},
   244	 leaf/.style={circle,draw,fill=green!15,minimum size=4mm}]
   245	\node[core,thick] (h) at (0,0) {$H$};
   246	\node[core] (c1) at (-1.5,0.8) {};
   247	\node[core] (c2) at (-1.5,-0.8) {};
   248	\node[core] (c3) at (0,1.5) {};
   249	\node[core] (c4) at (0,-1.5) {};
   250	\foreach \x/\y in {h/c1,h/c2,h/c3,h/c4,c1/c2,c1/c3,c2/c4,c3/c4}
   251	 \draw[blue!55] (\x)--(\y);
   252	\node[leaf] (l1) at (1.2,1.8) {};
   253	\node[leaf] (l2) at (1.8,1.2) {};
   254	\node[leaf] (l3) at (2.0,0.4) {};
   255	\foreach \x in {l1,l2,l3}\draw[green!50!black] (h)--(\x);
   256	\node[pair] (a1) at (4.0,1.0) {};
   257	\node[pair] (a2) at (5.0,1.0) {};
   258	\node[pair] (b1) at (4.0,-1.0) {};
   259	\node[pair] (b2) at (5.0,-1.0) {};
   260	\draw[orange!80!black,very thick] (a1)--node[above] {$C_t/\sigma_*$}(a2);
   261	\draw[orange!80!black,very thick] (b1)--(b2);
   262	\foreach \x in {a1,a2,b1,b2}{
   263	 \draw[gray!65!black,dashed] (\x)--(c3);
   264	 \draw[gray!65!black,dashed] (\x)--(c4);
   265	}
   266	\node[align=center] at (-1.7,-2.0) {unit clique\\$C_t=t^4$};
   267	\node[align=center] at (1.9,2.25) {$m_t$ hub leaves};
   268	\node[align=center] at (4.5,-1.75) {$q_t$ heavy pairs};
   269	\node[gray!65!black] at (3.2,0) {all weak edges $\eps_t$};
   270	\end{tikzpicture}
   271	\caption{Schematic of the dilute hybrid.  Dashed lines represent all
   272	satellite--clique edges, not only those drawn.}
   273	\label{fig:hybrid}
   274	\end{figure}
   275	
   276	To define the positive coupling without reference to a subsequently chosen
   277	fitness, let
   278	\[
   279	 I_t=[1+1/t,\Rhyb-1/t]
   280	\]
   281	when this interval is nonempty.  Let $\rho^0_{U,t}$ denote the finite
   282	separated trace defined in Section~\ref{sec:trace}.  Choose $e_t$ to be the
   283	least positive integer such that, for $\eps_t=2^{-e_t}$,
   284	\[
   285	 \sup_{r\in I_t}\frac{n_t}{q_t}
   286	 \left|
   287	 \frac{\rho_U(G_t(\eps_t),r)}{\rho_U(K_{n_t},r)}
   288	 -\frac{\rho^0_{U,t}(r)}{\rho_U(K_{n_t},r)}
   289	 \right|\leq\frac1t
   290	 \quad (U=\Bd,\dB).
   291	\]
   292	For the finitely many empty intervals, take $e_t=1$.
   293	
   294	\begin{lemma}[Effective dyadic diagonal]
   295	\label{lem:effective-diagonal}
   296	The integer $e_t$ exists and is computable by exact real-algebraic
   297	arithmetic.  In particular, the family $\{G_t\}$ is effectively defined and
   298	every one of its weights is fixed before fitness is quantified.
   299	\end{lemma}
   300	
   301	\begin{proof}
   302	Work over the algebraic number field $\mathbb K=\mathbb Q(\Rhyb)$.
   303	Both $\sigma_*$ and $\lambda_*$ lie in $\mathbb K$, and
   304	$m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor$ is obtained by an exact comparison of real
   305	algebraic numbers.  For fixed $t,e$ and either update rule, the transient
   306	first-step system of $G_t(2^{-e})$ has entries in $\mathbb K(r)$.  Cramer's
   307	rule therefore puts its fixation probability in $\mathbb K(r)$; the same is
   308	true of the finite separated trace and the complete-graph baseline.  For
   309	each $r\in I_t$, every transient block $Q(r)$ occurring in these absorbing
   310	systems is substochastic with spectral radius less than one.  Hence
   311	$I-Q(r)$ is a nonsingular $M$-matrix and
   312	$\det(I-Q(r))>0$; the event denominators are positive as well.
   313	
   314	After clearing those positive denominators, each of the two absolute-value
   315	conditions defining $e_t$ is a pair of polynomial inequalities over
   316	$\mathbb K$ quantified on the algebraic interval $I_t$.  Its truth is
   317	decidable by Sturm--subresultant arithmetic, equivalently by quantifier
   318	elimination in the real closure of $\mathbb K$.  Proposition
   319	\ref{prop:weak-trace} gives uniform convergence on $I_t$, so some exponent is
   320	admissible.  Testing $e=1,2,\ldots$ by the exact decision procedure therefore
   321	terminates at the least admissible $e_t$.
   322	\end{proof}
   323	
   324	\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]
   325	\label{thm:main}
   326	For every fixed $1<r<\Rhyb$, the family above satisfies, for every
   327	sufficiently large $t$,
   328	\[
   329	 \rho_{\Bd}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r),\qquad
   330	 \rho_{\dB}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r).
   331	\]
   332	Consequently
   333	\[
   334	 \boxed{\Rsim\geq\Rhyb=1.5028569127905696\ldots>3/2.}
   335	\]
   336	\end{theorem}
   337	
   338	\section{Exact lumping and the weak-cut limiting trace}
   339	\label{sec:trace}
   340	
   341	A configuration has orbit label
   342	\[
   343	 (h,i,u,v,\ell),
   344	\]
   345	where $h$ is the hub type, $i$ the number of mutant ordinary clique
   346	vertices, $u$ the number of mixed pairs, $v$ the number of all-mutant pairs,
   347	and $\ell$ the mutant-leaf count.  The group
   348	\[
   349	S_{C_t-1}\times(S_2\wr S_{q_t})\times S_{m_t}
   350	\]
   351	is transitive on every fibre and commutes with both update kernels.  This
   352	immediately gives the following exact reduction.
   353	
   354	\begin{lemma}[Strong orbit lumping]
   355	\label{lem:lumping}
   356	For every positive value of the weak-edge weight and for both update rules,
   357	the total transition probability from a labelled configuration to a target
   358	fibre depends only on $(h,i,u,v,\ell)$.
   359	\end{lemma}
   360	
   361	\begin{proof}
   362	Every permutation in the displayed wreath-product action preserves vertex
   363	degrees, edge weights, types, and fitnesses.  It therefore commutes with each
   364	labelled parent--target event under Bd and each labelled death--parent event
   365	under dB.  Summing events into a target fibre proves strong lumpability.
   366	\end{proof}
   367	
   368	We next fix finite $C,m,q$ and send $\eps\downarrow0$.  At $\eps=0$ the
   369	closed fast classes are precisely the states in which the center module and
   370	every pair are internally homogeneous.  Write a macro state as
   371	\[
   372	 (h,k)\in\{0,1\}\times\{0,\ldots,q\},
   373	\]
   374	where $h$ is the center type and $k$ the number of mutant pairs.  Partition
   375	the finite first-step matrix into mixed-module states $F$ and all
   376	monomorphic-module states $M$, explicitly including global extinction and
   377	fixation.  Their two harmonic values remain fixed at zero and one.
   378	
   379	\begin{proposition}[Finite-state weak-cut limiting trace]
   380	\label{prop:weak-trace}
   381	Let $a_H^U,a_P^U$ be the uniformly initialized isolated fixation
   382	probabilities of the center and one pair, and let $P_U^H,P_U^P$ be fixation
   383	probabilities of the limiting macro chain from $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$.  For
   384	$U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$,
   385	\begin{equation}
   386	 \rho_U^0(C,m,q;r)
   387	 =\frac{C+m}{N}a_H^U(r)P_U^H
   388	 +\frac{2q}{N}a_P^U(r)P_U^P,
   389	 \qquad N=C+m+2q.
   390	\label{eq:finite-trace}
   391	\end{equation}
   392	Moreover, $\rho_U(G(C,m,q,\eps),r)\to\rho_U^0(C,m,q;r)$ uniformly in $r$
   393	on every compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$.
   394	\end{proposition}
   395	
   396	\begin{proof}
   397	Order the states as $M,F$.  At $\eps=0$, the first-step matrix
   398	has the block form
   399	\[
   400	 P_U(0)=
   401	 \begin{pmatrix}I&0\\ C_0&Q_0\end{pmatrix},
   402	 \qquad \rho(Q_0)<1.
   403	\]
   404	Indeed, from a mixed state each finite connected module absorbs internally
   405	almost surely, so $I-Q_0$ is invertible with nonnegative inverse and
   406	$(I-Q_0)^{-1}C_0$ is the stochastic matrix of its first monomorphic return.
   407	Expanding the positive-cut chain gives
   408	\[
   409	 P_{MM}(\eps)=I+\eps A+O(\eps^2),\qquad
   410	 P_{MF}(\eps)=\eps B+O(\eps^2),
   411	\]
   412	and $P_{FM}(\eps)=C_0+O(\eps)$,
   413	$P_{FF}(\eps)=Q_0+O(\eps)$.  Eliminating $F$ from the finite harmonic
   414	system yields, after division by the common factor $\eps$, the macro
   415	generator
   416	\begin{equation}
   417	 \mathcal L_U=A+B(I-Q_0)^{-1}C_0.
   418	\label{eq:schur-trace}
   419	\end{equation}
   420	The second term first makes one cross-module introduction and then resolves
   421	its entire zero-cut internal excursion.  Its absorption distribution is
   422	therefore exactly the affected module's local fixation probability.  On the
   423	two globally absorbing macro states we retain boundary values zero and one;
   424	on every other macro state, the leading harmonic equation is
   425	$\mathcal L_Uh=0$.  Positive introduction rates make every interior state
   426	of this finite macro ladder transient.  On a compact fitness interval its
   427	interior inverse is uniformly bounded, so the boundary-value problem has a
   428	unique solution.  This is the claimed
   429	introduction-rate-times-fixation trace, derived from the finite chain rather
   430	than from independent lineages.
   431	
   432	A uniformly chosen initial mutant first resolves inside its center or pair
   433	module with probability $1-O(\eps)$.  Conditioning on which module fixes
   434	locally gives \eqref{eq:finite-trace}.  All matrices are finite and their
   435	entries are continuous rational functions of $(\eps,r)$ with positive event
   436	denominators.  After fixing the two boundary coordinates, the scaled Schur
   437	complement converges to the invertible interior restriction of
   438	$\mathcal L_U$.  On a compact fitness interval that limiting inverse,
   439	$(I-Q_0)^{-1}$, and the nearby absorbing inverses are uniformly bounded.
   440	The block expansion and hence the convergence are uniform there.
   441	\end{proof}
   442	
   443	\section{Clique--pendant estimates at the gain scale}
   444	
   445	Let $H_{C,m}$ be a unit clique $K_C$ with $m$ unit hub pendants and put
   446	$p=1-1/r$.  Along $C=t^4$, $q=t$, and $m\asymp t$, the following estimates hold
   447	uniformly when $r$ ranges over a compact subset of $(1,\infty)$:
   448	\begin{proposition}[Center module]
   449	\label{prop:center}
   450	\begin{align}
   451	 u_{\rm core}^U(r)&=p+o(q/C),\qquad U=\Bd,\dB,\label{eq:core}\\
   452	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)&=1-o(1),\label{eq:leaf-Bd}\\
   453	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)&=O(C^{-1}).\label{eq:leaf-dB}
   454	\end{align}
   455	Here $u_{\rm core}^U$ averages the $C$ clique starting vertices.
   456	Consequently
   457	\begin{align}
   458	 a_H^{\Bd}(r)&=\frac{Cp+m}{C+m}+o(q/C),\label{eq:center-Bd}\\
   459	 a_H^{\dB}(r)&=\frac{Cp}{C+m}+o(q/C).\label{eq:center-dB}
   460	\end{align}
   461	At reciprocal fitness $1/r$,
   462	\[
   463	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),\qquad J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),
   464	\]
   465	where $J_H$ is the dB portal functional defined below.
   466	\end{proposition}
   467	
   468	We prove the proposition through five stopped-process lemmas.  This detail is
   469	needed because an $o(1)$ establishment approximation would be too weak: the
   470	final gain is only of order $q/C$.  Fix throughout a compact interval
   471	$\mathcal R=[r_-,r_+]\Subset(1,\infty)$.  Write a center state as
   472	$(h,i,\ell)$, put $c=C-1$, $d=c+m$, and suppress a common positive statewise
   473	time factor.  The six Bd changing rates are
   474	\[
   475	\begin{array}{c|c}
   476	\text{change}&\text{rate}\\ \hline
   477	i\to i+1&r(c-i)\{h/d+i/c\}\\
   478	i\to i-1&i\{(1-h)/d+(c-i)/c\}\\
   479	0\to1\text{ at }h&r(i/c+\ell)\\
   480	1\to0\text{ at }h&(c-i)/c+m-\ell\\
   481	\ell\to\ell+1&rh(m-\ell)/d\\
   482	\ell\to\ell-1&(1-h)\ell/d .
   483	\end{array}
   484	\]
   485	After multiplication by $C+m$, the six dB rates are
   486	\[
   487	\begin{array}{c|c}
   488	i\to i+1&(c-i)\dfrac{r(h+i)}{c+(r-1)(h+i)}\\[1mm]
   489	i\to i-1&i\dfrac{c-i+1-h}{c+(r-1)(i-1+h)}\\[1mm]
   490	0\to1\text{ at }h&\dfrac{r(i+\ell)}{d+(r-1)(i+\ell)}\\[1mm]
   491	1\to0\text{ at }h&\dfrac{d-i-\ell}{d+(r-1)(i+\ell)}\\[1mm]
   492	\ell\to\ell+1&h(m-\ell)\\
   493	\ell\to\ell-1&(1-h)\ell .
   494	\end{array}
   495	\]
   496	These tables follow by summing the labelled parent--target and
   497	death--parent events and are the only estimates used below.
   498	
   499	\begin{lemma}[Early ordinary-core establishment]
   500	\label{lem:early-establishment}
   501	Start from one ordinary clique mutant, with resident hub and no mutant
   502	pendants.  Let $\chi$ be the first change of the hub or pendant coordinates,
   503	let $K=\lceil A_0\log C\rceil$, and let
   504	$\tau_j=\inf\{s:i_s=j\}$ and
   505	$\tau_{0,K}=\tau_0\wedge\tau_K$.  Uniformly for
   506	$r\in\mathcal R$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$,
   507	\begin{align}
   508	 \Prob(\chi<\tau_{0,K})&=O(K^2/C),\label{eq:early-mark}\\
   509	 \Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)
   510	 &=1-\frac1r+O(K^2/C)+O(r^{-K}).\label{eq:early-est}
   511	\end{align}
   512	The constants are uniform for every fixed $A_0$.
   513	\end{lemma}
   514	
   515	\begin{proof}
   516	Until $\chi$, the exact embedded ordinary-count up/down odds are
   517	\begin{align}
   518	 R_{\Bd}(i)
   519	 &=r\,\frac{c-i}{c-i+c/(c+m)},\\
   520	 R_{\dB}(i)
   521	 &=r\,\frac{c-i}{c-i+1}
   522	 \frac{c+(r-1)(i-1)}{c+(r-1)i}.
   523	\end{align}
   524	Thus $\lambda_i^U:=(R_U(i))^{-1}=r^{-1}\{1+O(K/C)\}$ uniformly for
   525	$1\leq i<K$.  For large $C$, the embedded ordinary walk has conditional
   526	upward probability at least $1/2+\eta$ for some $\eta>0$ depending only on
   527	$\mathcal R$.  If $N$ is its number of changes before $\{0,K\}$, optional
   528	stopping applied to the count minus its uniform positive drift gives
   529	$\mathbb E_1N\leq K/(2\eta)=O(K)$.  At every visited state the hub-change
   530	hazard divided by the ordinary-change hazard is $O(K/C)$; a union bound over
   531	the stopped changes gives \eqref{eq:early-mark}.  Pendant coordinates cannot
   532	change before the initially resident hub does.
   533	
   534	Suppressing that killing event, the product-odds formula is
   535	\[
   536	 \Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)
   537	 =\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\prod_{j=1}^k\lambda_j^U\right)^{-1}.
   538	\]
   539	For $k<K$, the product is
   540	$r^{-k}\exp\{O(kK/C)\}$; summing the geometric tail and restoring the
   541	killing event changes the answer by
   542	$O(K^2/C)+O(r^{-K})$.  This proves \eqref{eq:early-est}.
   543	\end{proof}
   544	\begin{lemma}[Supercritical completion and core confinement]
   545	\label{lem:core-confinement}
   546	There are constants $\delta>0$, $r_0>1$, $a,b,\gamma>0$, and an integer
   547	$R_0$, depending only on $\mathcal R$, with the following properties.
   548	Uniformly in the hub and pendant coordinates, a core family starting at
   549	$i=K$ reaches $i\geq(1-\delta)c$ before extinction except with probability
   550	\[
   551	 O(r_0^{-K})+e^{-\gamma C}.
   552	\]
   553	If $R=c-i$ and $R(0)\leq\delta c$, then for every fixed $M$,
   554	\begin{equation}
   555	 \Prob\!\left(\sup_{s\leq C^M}R_s\geq2\delta c\right)
   556	 \leq C_M C^{M+1}e^{-\gamma C},
   557	\label{eq:core-confinement}
   558	\end{equation}
   559	where time is measured in the displayed continuous-time clock.  Before the
   560	exceptional exit, the bounded strip is reached with the uniform tail
   561	\begin{equation}
   562	 \Prob\bigl(\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}>a\log C+x,
   563	             \tau_{2\delta c}>\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}\bigr)
   564	 \leq a e^{-bx}.
   565	\label{eq:deficit-return}
   566	\end{equation}
   567	\end{lemma}
   568	
   569	\begin{proof}
   570	For every hub and pendant state, the ordinary-count odds remain bounded
   571	below by a constant $r_0>1$ from $K$ to a fixed positive core density.
   572	Indeed, the two tables give the uniform lower bounds
   573	\[
   574	 \frac{q^+_{\Bd}}{q^-_{\Bd}}
   575	 \geq\frac{r}{1+c/\{d(c-i)\}},\qquad
   576	 \frac{q^+_{\dB}}{q^-_{\dB}}
   577	 =r\,\frac{h+i}{i}\frac{c-i}{c-i+1-h}
   578	 \frac{c+(r-1)(i-1+h)}{c+(r-1)(i+h)}.
   579	\]
   580	For $K\to\infty$ and $i\leq(1-\delta)c$, their infimum is strictly larger
   581	than one.  This comparison remains valid although the hidden coordinates
   582	move.  At successive $i$-change epochs, condition on the complete past.  The
   583	pointwise intensity ratio implies that the conditional probability of an
   584	upward step is at least $r_0/(1+r_0)$: this follows equivalently by comparing
   585	the two compensator integrals up to the next $i$-change.  Thus
   586	$r_0^{-i_n}$ is a supermartingale for the embedded adapted process.  Stopping
   587	on $\{0,\lfloor(1-\delta)c\rfloor\}$ gives
   588	\[
   589	 \Prob_K\bigl(\tau_0<\tau_{\lfloor(1-\delta)c\rfloor}\bigr)
   590	 \leq r_0^{-K}.
   591	\]
   592	Applying the same conditional-bias comparison across any subsequent
   593	fixed-density interval gives an $e^{-\gamma C}$ failure bound.
   594	
   595	In the upper strip $1\leq R\leq2\delta c$, the same tables give, uniformly
   596	in the hub and pendant coordinates,
   597	\begin{equation}
   598	 \frac{q(R\mapsto R-1)}{q(R\mapsto R+1)}
   599	 \geq r(1-2\delta)\frac{R}{R+1}\{1-o(1)\}.
   600	\label{eq:deficit-odds}
   601	\end{equation}
   602	Writing $r_-$ for the lower end of the chosen fitness compact, choose
   603	\[
   604	 \delta<\min\left\{\frac18,\frac{r_--1}{8r_-},
   605	 \frac{r_-^2-1}{4(r_-^2+1)}\right\}.
   606	\]
   607	Then one may take $\alpha=(r_-+1)/2>1$ and, for all sufficiently large
   608	$C$, replace the right side of \eqref{eq:deficit-odds} by
   609	$\alpha R/(R+1)$.  It is larger than $1+\eta_0$ above the fixed integer
   610	$R_0=\lceil(\alpha+1)/(\alpha-1)\rceil$; no statewise drift assertion is
   611	made below $R_0$.  The hidden hub and pendant coordinates do not make the
   612	deficit marginal Markov, so we use a conditional-bias coupling rather than
   613	assuming that they do.  At successive $R$-change epochs, condition on the
   614	entire past.  Whenever $R>R_0$, the probability of an outward step divided
   615	by that of an inward step is at most $(1+\eta_0)^{-1}$.  Choose
   616	$0<\theta<\log(1+\eta_0)$.  Then
   617	\[
   618	 \exp\{\theta R_{n\wedge\tau}\},\qquad
   619	 \tau=\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}},
   620	\]
   621	is a supermartingale for the embedded adapted process.  Optional stopping
   622	therefore gives, uniformly in all hidden-coordinate histories,
   623	\begin{equation}
   624	 \sup_{k\leq\delta c}
   625	 \Prob_k(\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}<\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}})
   626	 \leq e^{-\theta\delta c}=e^{-\gamma C}.
   627	\label{eq:deficit-initial-excursion}
   628	\end{equation}
   629	The same estimate, started at $R_0+1$, controls every later excursion.
   630	The total $R$-change intensity in the displayed clock is at most $\Lambda C$.
   631	Its compensator through time $C^M$ is therefore at most
   632	$\Lambda C^{M+1}$.  Applying the strong Markov property at successive
   633	entries into $R_0+1$ and multiplying the last excursion bound by the expected
   634	number of entries proves \eqref{eq:core-confinement}; Markov's inequality for
   635	the compensator already suffices.
   636	
   637	For completeness, the stopped generator calculation behind the return-time
   638	claim is also explicit.  Substituting $i=c-R$ in the two tables gives
   639	\begin{align*}
   640	 \mathcal G_{\Bd}R
   641	 &=(c-R)\left\{\frac{1-h}{d}+\frac Rc\right\}
   642	   -rR\left\{\frac hd+\frac{c-R}{c}\right\},\\
   643	 \mathcal G_{\dB}R
   644	 &=(c-R)\frac{R+1-h}{c+(r-1)(c-R-1+h)}
   645	   -R\frac{r(c-R+h)}{c+(r-1)(c-R+h)}.
   646	\end{align*}
   647	After increasing $R_0$ and decreasing $\delta$ if necessary, both are at
   648	most $-\kappa R$ for $R_0<R<2\delta c$, uniformly in $h,\ell$, and
   649	$r\in\mathcal R$.  Thus
   650	$e^{\kappa(s\wedge\tau)}R_{s\wedge\tau}$ is a supermartingale in the
   651	displayed clock.  On the event in \eqref{eq:deficit-return} it has not yet
   652	hit either boundary at time $a\log C+x$.  Hence
   653	\[
   654	 \Prob(\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}>a\log C+x,
   655	       \tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}>\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}})
   656	 \leq \frac{\delta C}{R_0}e^{-\kappa(a\log C+x)}.
   657	\]
   658	Taking $a>2/\kappa$ proves \eqref{eq:deficit-return}.  The states below
   659	$R_0$ form the announced boundary layer and require no statewise drift claim.
   660	\end{proof}
   661	
   662	\begin{lemma}[Pendant synchronization and pendant initialization]
   663	\label{lem:pendant-cleanup}
   664	For every fixed $B_0>0$ there is a fixed $D_{\rm cl}=D_{\rm cl}(B_0)$ such
   665	that, under either update
   666	rule and uniformly in $r\in\mathcal R$, a state with
   667	$i\geq(1-\delta)c$ reaches fixation within time $C^{D_{\rm cl}}$, except with
   668	probability
   669	\[
   670	 O(C^{-B_0})+O(C^{D_{\rm cl}+1}e^{-\gamma C}).
   671	\]
   672	Moreover, fixation from one mutant pendant satisfies
   673	\[
   674	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1),\qquad
   675	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1}).
   676	\]
   677	\end{lemma}
   678	
   679	\begin{proof}
   680	We first synchronize the hub and its pendants while the core is confined.
   681	Under Bd, freeze $(i,R,\ell)$ between hub and pendant events and put
   682	\[
   683	 H_+=r(i/c+\ell),\quad H_-=R/c+m-\ell,
   684	 \quad U_\ell=\frac{r(m-\ell)}{c+m},\quad
   685	 V_\ell=\frac{\ell}{c+m}.
   686	\]
   687	If $p_h$ is the probability that the next leaf event is upward, conditional
   688	on the current hub type $h$, renewal at the next hub flip gives
   689	\[
   690	 p_0=\frac{H_+}{H_++V_\ell}p_1,\qquad
   691	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell}{H_-+U_\ell}
   692	     +\frac{H_-}{H_-+U_\ell}p_0.
   693	\]
   694	To make the interruption comparison formal, replace these rates by
   695	\[
   696	 \underline H_+=r_-(1-2\delta+\ell),\qquad
   697	 \overline H_-=2\delta+m-\ell,
   698	 \qquad \underline U_\ell=\frac{r_-(m-\ell)}{c+m},
   699	\]
   700	and let $(\underline p_0,\underline p_1)$ solve the same two equations with
   701	these worst rates and $V_\ell$.  The resulting two-state hitting function is
   702	subharmonic for the full $(h,i,\ell)$ generator until the next pendant-count
   703	change: core jumps leave it unchanged, while
   704	$H_+\geq\underline H_+$, $H_-\leq\overline H_-$, and
   705	$U_\ell\geq\underline U_\ell$ move its generator in the favorable direction.
   706	Indeed, the two renewal equations give
   707	\[
   708	 p_0=\frac{H_+U_\ell}
   709	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)},\qquad
   710	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell(H_++V_\ell)}
   711	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)};
   712	\]
   713	direct differentiation shows that both are increasing in $H_+$ and
   714	$U_\ell$ and decreasing in $H_-$.  This proves the asserted favorable
   715	monotonicity rather than leaving it implicit.
   716	Optional stopping therefore makes it a lower bound even with an adapted
   717	sequence of core interruptions.  Its next-leaf up/down odds, from either hub
   718	phase, are bounded below by
   719	\begin{equation}
   720	 \frac{\underline H_+\underline U_\ell}
   721	      {\overline H_-V_\ell}
   722	 \frac1{1+\underline U_\ell/\overline H_-}
   723	 \geq\frac{r_-^2}{1+2\delta}\{1-o(1)\}>1.
   724	\label{eq:leaf-trace-odds}
   725	\end{equation}
   726	for $1\leq\ell<m$.
   727	
   728	The calendar-time estimate can be checked without suppressing core events.
   729	During an $h=0$ phase the activation-or-loss intensity is bounded below by a
   730	positive constant, and during an $h=1$ phase the deactivation-or-gain
   731	intensity is at least $m-\ell\geq1$.  Moreover,
   732	\begin{align*}
   733	 \Prob(\text{loss before activation}\mid h=0)
   734	 &\geq \frac{V_\ell}{r_+(1+\ell)+V_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},\\
   735	 \Prob(\text{gain before deactivation}\mid h=1)
   736	 &\geq\frac{\underline U_\ell}
   737	 {2\delta+m-\ell+\underline U_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C}.
   738	\end{align*}
   739	Core jumps contribute zero to functions of $(h,\ell)$ and do not pause these
   740	competing clocks.  Hence the number of hub phases before the next pendant
   741	change is dominated by a geometric variable of mean $O(C)$, and each phase
   742	has uniformly bounded mean duration.  Thus
   743	\begin{equation}
   744	 \mathbb E(\sigma_{j+1}-\sigma_j\mid\mathcal F_{\sigma_j})
   745	 \leq K_0C,
   746	\label{eq:leaf-wait}
   747	\end{equation}
   748	where $\sigma_j$ are successive pendant-count changes.
   749	Thus the pendant trace is dominated below by a reflected walk whose
   750	up/down odds are at least a fixed $\beta>1$.  It makes $O(m)$ expected
   751	changes before reaching $m$; together with \eqref{eq:leaf-wait},
   752	\begin{equation}
   753	 \sup_{R\leq2\delta c}\mathbb E\tau_{\{\ell=m\}}=O(Cm).
   754	\label{eq:leaf-hitting-time}
   755	\end{equation}
   756	
   757	At $\ell=m$, a resident hub activates before the next pendant loss with
   758	probability $1-O(C^{-1})$.  After activation, first suppress hub loss and
   759	keep $\ell=m$.  The Bd deficit generator is then exactly
   760	\[
   761	 \mathcal G R
   762	 =-R\left\{\frac r{c+m}+(r-1)\left(1-\frac Rc\right)\right\}.
   763	\]
   764	After shrinking $\delta$ once, the bracket is at least a compact-uniform
   765	$\kappa>0$ on $R\leq3\delta c$.  Starting from $R\leq2\delta c$ and stopping
   766	at $\tau=\tau_0\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}$, Dynkin's formula gives
   767	\[
   768	 \mathbb E\int_0^\tau R_s\,ds\leq\frac{R(0)}\kappa
   769	 \leq\frac{2\delta c}\kappa.
   770	\]
   771	The same adapted step calculation, now with $3\delta$ in place of
   772	$2\delta$, also gives
   773	$\Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}<\tau_0)\leq e^{-\gamma C}$.  Conditional
   774	on the suppressed path, the probability of no hub loss is
   775	$\exp\{-\int_0^\tau R_s/c\,ds\}$.  Jensen's inequality and subtraction of
   776	the upper-exit event therefore yield
   777	\begin{equation}
   778	 \Prob(\hbox{no hub loss and }\tau_0<\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}})
   779	 \geq e^{-2\delta/\kappa}-e^{-\gamma C}=:s_1+o(1)>0.
   780	\label{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}
   781	\end{equation}
   782	The same exponential Lyapunov estimate makes this final cleanup occur within
   783	$C^2$ with probability $1-o(1)$.  Together with
   784	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time} and $m=o(C)$, Markov's inequality gives a fixed
   785	$s_0>0$ chance of full cleanup in a block of $2C^2$ time.  If hub loss ends
   786	an unsuccessful final attempt, the same adapted deficit comparison, now
   787	using the nested strips $2\delta c<3\delta c<4\delta c$ after shrinking
   788	$\delta$, returns the process to $R\leq\delta c$ in polynomial time before
   789	the next block; escape to $4\delta c$ before that return costs
   790	$e^{-\gamma C}$.  Starting the next block from $R\leq\delta c$ puts it back
   791	under the $2\delta c$ confinement estimate.  The earlier
   792	hit of $3\delta c$ in \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup} is charged to this same
   793	exceptional event rather than recycled as a new block.  We now formalize the
   794	repetition.
   795	
   796	Here is the stopping-time regeneration behind that last sentence.  Put
   797	$\mathcal E_C=\{R\leq\delta c\}$, write $\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}$ for the
   798	fixation time, and let $S_1$ be the initial time (or the first entry into
   799	$\mathcal E_C$).  Given $S_j$, run the following block:
   800	put
   801	\[
   802	 \Sigma_j=\inf\{s\geq S_j:(h_s,\ell_s)=(1,m)
   803	                         \text{ or }R_s\geq2\delta c\}.
   804	\]
   805	At each visit to $\ell=m$ with resident hub, activation precedes the next
   806	pendant loss with probability $1-O(C^{-1})$; after a loss the same pendant
   807	trace restarts.  Thus \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time} also gives
   808	$\mathbb E(\Sigma_j-S_j\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})\leq K C m$, with a hit of
   809	$2\delta c$ charged as exceptional.  From a nonexceptional $\Sigma_j$, run
   810	the final cleanup until fixation, hub loss, or a hit of $3\delta c$;
   811	and, after an ordinary hub-loss failure below $3\delta c$, wait for the
   812	first return to $\mathcal E_C$, charging a hit of $4\delta c$ as exceptional.
   813	Let $T_j$ be the time of fixation, exceptional escape, or this return.  On
   814	an ordinary return set $S_{j+1}=T_j$; a hit of $3\delta c$ is charged rather
   815	than recycled.  For that return, shrink $\delta$ so that
   816	$\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ on $\delta c<R<4\delta c$, and put
   817	$\rho=\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}}\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}$.
   818	The same adapted-step and generator arguments give, from $R<3\delta c$,
   819	\[
   820	 \Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}<\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}})
   821	 \leq e^{-\gamma C},\qquad
   822	 \Prob(\rho>t)\leq3e^{-\kappa t}.
   823	\]
   824	Equations
   825	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}, \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}, and
   826	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}, together with this nested-strip estimate, give
   827	constants $s_*>0$, $D_*=D_*(B_0)$, and $\gamma_*>0$ such that, uniformly on
   828	$\mathcal F_{S_j}$,
   829	\begin{align}
   830	 \Prob(\hbox{fixation during block }j,\ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}
   831	       \mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   832	 &\geq s_*,\label{eq:Bd-block-success}\\
   833	 \Prob(\hbox{upper-strip escape during block }j,
   834	       \ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   835	 &\leq K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C},\label{eq:Bd-block-escape}\\
   836	 \Prob(T_j-S_j>C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   837	 &\leq C^{-B_0-2}.
   838	 \label{eq:Bd-block-duration}
   839	\end{align}
   840	For the last bound, the synchronization part has conditional mean $O(Cm)$
   841	by \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}; the final attempt and return part has
   842	conditional mean $O(C^2)$ by the stopped Lyapunov estimates.  Markov's
   843	inequality gives \eqref{eq:Bd-block-duration} after taking, for example,
   844	$D_*=B_0+4$.  With
   845	\[
   846	 N_C=\left\lceil
   847	 \frac{(B_0+2)\log C}{-\log(1-s_*)}\right\rceil,
   848	\]
   849	the strong Markov property at the $S_j$ gives
   850	\[
   851	 \Prob(\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}>N_CC^{D_*})
   852	 \leq(1-s_*)^{N_C}
   853	   +N_C\{C^{-B_0-2}+K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C}\}
   854	 =O(C^{-B_0}).
   855	\]
   856	Moreover $N_CC^{D_*}\leq C^{D_{\rm cl}}$ for a fixed
   857	$D_{\rm cl}=D_{\rm cl}(B_0)$.  This proves the
   858	claimed Bd time and failure bounds without an implicit independence
   859	assumption between blocks.
   860	
   861	Under dB, every pendant copies the hub at its next death.  In the bounded
   862	$R$ strip, first wait for $R\leq R_0$ using
   863	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}.  The hub activation rate is then bounded below.
   864	During a mutant-hub interval, its deactivation intensity is at most
   865	$K(R+m-\ell)/C$.  Suppressing that deactivation, direct substitution in the
   866	dB table gives $\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ while $R\leq2\delta c$, and the
   867	number $m-\ell$ of resident pendants is a pure-death chain with drift
   868	$-(m-\ell)$.  Choose a fixed $\beta_0=\beta_0(B_0)>0$ such that
   869	\[
   870	 \beta_0-\frac14\geq B_0+2,
   871	 \qquad
   872	 \kappa\beta_0\geq B_0+2,
   873	\]
   874	and put $T=\beta_0\log C$.  Then
   875	\begin{align*}
   876	 \Prob(\ell_T<m)&\leq me^{-T},\\
   877	 \Prob(R_T>0,\ \tau_\uparrow>T)
   878	 &\leq R_0e^{-\kappa T},\\
   879	 \Prob(\text{hub deactivates before }T\wedge\tau_\uparrow)
   880	 &\leq \mathbb E\int_0^{T\wedge\tau_\uparrow}
   881	 K\frac{R_s+m-\ell_s}{C}\,ds
   882	   \leq\frac K C\left(\frac{R_0}{\kappa}+m\right)=o(1).
   883	\end{align*}
   884	Here $\tau_\uparrow=\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}$; its omitted upper-strip exit
   885	has probability $e^{-\gamma C}$.  Since $m=O(C^{1/4})$, the choice of
   886	$\beta_0$ makes the first two displayed probabilities $O(C^{-B_0-2})$,
   887	whereas hub survival has probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus an activated attempt
   888	started in $R\leq R_0$ succeeds with
   889	probability at least $1/2$ for large $C$.  After a failed attempt, confinement
   890	and \eqref{eq:deficit-return} return the process to $R\leq R_0$ in
   891	polynomial time; unless fixation has already occurred, the hub is activated
   892	again at a rate bounded below.  The strong Markov property over
   893	$O_{B_0}(\log C)$ such attempts gives failure $O(C^{-B_0})$, while their
   894	total duration is polynomial.  Taking $D_{\rm cl}$ larger than the two block
   895	exponents and using \eqref{eq:core-confinement} proves the first assertion.
   896	
   897	For a Bd mutant leaf, use the standard weighted graphical construction: each
   898	vertex $u$ has a base reproduction clock of rate one and, when it rings,
   899	targets $v$ with probability $w_{uv}/d_u$; a mutant $u$ has an independent
   900	extra clock of rate $r-1$ with the same target law.  Deleting mutants can
   901	only decrease the mutant set.  Fix the original mutant pendant.  At every
   902	regeneration with resident hub, no ordinary mutants, and that pendant still
   903	alive, delete all other mutants and suppress births to the other pendants.
   904	This produces the adverse lower state $(h,i,\ell)=(0,0,1)$.
   905	In its resident-hub phase, activation has rate $r$ and loss of the
   906	distinguished pendant has rate $1/d$, so loss before activation is
   907	$O(C^{-1})$.  In the ensuing mutant-hub phase, an ordinary seed has rate at
   908	least $rc/d\geq a_0$, while hub loss has rate $m$; hence a seed precedes hub
   909	loss with probability at least $a_1/m$.
   910	
   911	After a seed, stop the ordinary count on $\{0,K\}$.  For every hidden hub
   912	and pendant state, the Bd up/down odds on $1\leq i<K$ are at least a fixed
   913	$r_1>1$.  The conditional-bias argument therefore gives
   914	$\Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)\geq p_0>0$.  The embedded walk makes $O(K)$ expected
   915	changes, and its changing intensity is bounded below, so
   916	$\mathbb E\tau_{0,K}=O(K)$.  The distinguished-pendant loss intensity is at
   917	most $1/d$, giving $O(K/C)$ loss during this stopped trial.  If the count
   918	hits zero while the pendant survives, wait for resident-hub regeneration,
   919	suppressing or deleting every ordinary reseed and every additional mutant
   920	pendant until hub loss.  This is again an adverse graphical coupling: the
   921	distinguished pendant cannot be lost while its sole neighbor is mutant, and
   922	any subsequent resident-phase loss is charged as trial failure.  Delete the
   923	extra mutants again at regeneration.  If it hits $K$, the preceding completion
   924	argument fixes with probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus a trial succeeds with
   925	probability at least $a_2/m$, whereas it loses the distinguished pendant
   926	with probability $O((1+K)/C)$.  The elementary renewal inequality for these
   927	competing outcomes gives loss before success
   928	$O(m(1+K)/C)=o(1)$.  Hence $u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1)$.
   929	Under dB, the leaf dies at rate one after the common death-clock time change,
   930	whereas hub activation is at most $r_+/C$.  Fixation requires that activation,
   931	so $u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1})$.
   932	\end{proof}
   933	
   934	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal killed-Green bounds]
   935	\label{lem:killed-green}
   936	Put $s=1/r$ and suppress hub changes while $h=0,\ell=0$.  Stop the resulting
   937	ordinary-count chain $I$ on hitting $0$ or $\lceil\delta c\rceil$, and define
   938	\[
   939	 G_C^U(i,j)=\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
   940	             \mathbf1_{\{I_z=j\}}\,dz.
   941	\]
   942	For every fixed $L$ there are $a_L,\kappa,\theta>0$, uniform for
   943	$s\in[1/r_+,1/r_-]$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$, such that
   944	\begin{align}
   945	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i(\tau_{\lceil\delta c\rceil}<\tau_0)
   946	 &\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C},\label{eq:green-macro}\\
   947	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_jjG_C^U(i,j)&\leq a_L,
   948	 \label{eq:green-mass}\\
   949	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_{j>L'}jG_C^U(i,j)
   950	 &\leq a_Le^{-\theta L'}.
   951	 \label{eq:green-tail}
   952	\end{align}
   953	Consequently the probability of a hub change before ordinary extinction is
   954	$O_L(C^{-1})$, and the ordinary count at that change has an exponentially
   955	decreasing tail after multiplication by $C$.
   956	\end{lemma}
   957	
   958	\begin{proof}
   959	For $i\leq\delta c$, the reciprocal-fitness versions of the two rate tables
   960	give
   961	\[
   962	 \frac{q_i^+}{q_i^-}\leq s_+\{1+O(\delta)+O(C^{-1})\}\leq\vartheta<1
   963	\]
   964	after decreasing $\delta$, where $s_+=1/r_-$.  They also give
   965	$q_i^--q_i^+\geq\kappa_0i$ in the displayed clock.  The product-odds formula
   966	for the dominated nearest-neighbor walk proves \eqref{eq:green-macro}.
   967	Dynkin's formula for $I_{z\wedge\tau}$ gives
   968	\[
   969	 \kappa_0\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_z\,dz\leq i,
   970	\]
   971	which is \eqref{eq:green-mass}.  To obtain \eqref{eq:green-tail}, let $\nu$
   972	be the first hit of $L'+1$.  Conditional bias gives
   973	$\Prob_i(\nu<\tau_0)\leq K_L\vartheta^{L'-L}$.  After $\nu$, the same Dynkin
   974	estimate bounds the expected remaining particle-time by
   975	$(L'+1)/\kappa_0$.  Therefore
   976	\[
   977	 \mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
   978	 \leq \frac{K_L(L'+1)}{\kappa_0}\vartheta^{L'-L}
   979	 \leq a_Le^{-\theta L'},
   980	\]
   981	after decreasing $\theta$.
   982	
   983	With resident hub and no mutant pendants, the hub-change intensity in either
   984	rule is at most $K_1I_z/C$.  If $\chi$ is that first change, the two precise
   985	consequences are
   986	\begin{align}
   987	 \Prob_i(\chi<\tau)&\leq \frac{K_1a_L}{C},\label{eq:green-hub-change}\\
   988	 \Prob_i(\chi<\tau,\ I_\chi>L')
   989	 &\leq\frac{K_1}{C}\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
   990	 I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
   991	 \leq\frac{K_1a_L}{C}e^{-\theta L'}.
   992	\label{eq:green-hub-tail}
   993	\end{align}
   994	This proves the asserted change-event bounds.
   995	\end{proof}
   996	
   997	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal hub-excursion renewal]
   998	\label{lem:reciprocal-renewal}
   999	Uniformly for $r\in\mathcal R$,
  1000	\[
  1001	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),\qquad
  1002	 J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1}).
  1003	\]
  1004	\end{lemma}
  1005	
  1006	\begin{proof}
  1007	For fixed $L$, let $X_L$ be the states with resident hub, no mutant pendants,
  1008	and at most $L$ mutant ordinary vertices; let $Y_L$ be the analogous states
  1009	with mutant hub.  Write $x^U_{C,L}$ and $y^U_{C,L}$ for the largest fixation
  1010	probabilities on these two sets.  Lemma~\ref{lem:killed-green} first gives
  1011	the coarse bound
  1012	\begin{equation}
  1013	 x^U_{C,L}\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C}+a_L/C=O_L(C^{-1}).
  1014	\label{eq:renewal-coarse}
  1015	\end{equation}
  1016	
  1017	We now make the hub-excursion stopping decomposition explicit.  For Bd,
  1018	start from $Y_L$.  The hub-loss rate is at least $m$, while the total
  1019	ordinary-changing rate is $O_L(1)$ and the pendant-seeding rate is $O(m/C)$.
  1020	If hub loss is the first such event, the return state lies in $X_L$ exactly;
  1021	charging every other first event as successful fixation gives
  1022	\begin{equation}
  1023	 y^{\Bd}_{C,L}\leq x^{\Bd}_{C,L}+K_Lm^{-1}+K C^{-1}=o(1).
  1024	\label{eq:renewal-y-Bd}
  1025	\end{equation}
  1026	
  1027	For dB, let $\zeta$ be the first hub loss.  Until the ordinary count reaches
  1028	$\delta c$, the hub-loss intensity is bounded below explicitly:
  1029	\[
  1030	 q_{h:1\mapsto0}
  1031	 =\frac{d-i-\ell}{d+(s-1)(i+\ell)}
  1032	 \geq\frac{(1-\delta)c}{d}=:k_h>0.
  1033	\]
  1034	While the hub is mutant, the ordinary count is dominated by a
  1035	subcritical birth--death process with immigration:
  1036	\[
  1037	 q_i^+\leq b(i+1),\qquad q_i^-\geq d_0i,\qquad b<d_0,
  1038	\]
  1039	after decreasing $\delta$.  A common-clock monotone coupling gives
  1040	$I_s\leq\bar I_s$ for $s\leq\zeta$ until the macro boundary, where $\bar I$ has
  1041	birth rate $b(i+1)$ and death rate $d_0i$.  To make its maximum bound
  1042	explicit, choose $1<z<d_0/b$ and put $V(i)=z^i$.  Its generator satisfies
  1043	\[
  1044	 \overline{\mathcal G}V(i)
  1045	 =(z-1)z^i\left[b-\left(\frac{d_0}{z}-b\right)i\right]
  1046	 \leq K_z-\alpha_zV(i)
  1047	\]
  1048	for suitable compact-uniform $K_z,\alpha_z>0$.  If
  1049	$\bar\tau_M=\inf\{s:\bar I_s\geq M\}$, Dynkin's formula stopped at
  1050	$T\wedge\bar\tau_M$ gives
  1051	\begin{equation}
  1052	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i\!\left(\sup_{s\leq T}\bar I_s\geq M\right)
  1053	 \leq (z^L+K_zT)z^{-M}.
  1054	\label{eq:immigration-maximum}
  1055	\end{equation}
  1056	Consequently the ordinary cloud before $\zeta$ is uniformly tight.
  1057	Indeed, for fixed $T$ and $L\leq L_1<\delta c$,
  1058	\[
  1059	 \Prob\!\left(\sup_{s<\zeta}I_s>L_1\right)
  1060	 \leq e^{-k_hT}+(z^L+K_zT)z^{-L_1}
  1061	 =:\eta_L(L_1,T),
  1062	\]
  1063	because on the event that the cloud stays below $L_1$ through time $T$, the
  1064	hub-loss intensity remains at least $k_h$ throughout.  Thus the first term
  1065	is precisely the surviving-hub part of the finite-$T$ split; no
  1066	infinite-horizon maximum estimate is being used.
  1067	For fixed $T$, let $L_1\to\infty$ and then let $T\to\infty$; the bound
  1068	vanishes in that order,
  1069	uniformly in $C$ and reciprocal fitness.
  1070	
  1071	On the complementary event, suppress hub reactivation after $\zeta$.
  1072	The mutant pendants are then pure deaths, so
  1073	$\mathbb E\int_0^\infty\ell_s\,ds\leq m$; the killed-Green bound gives
  1074	$\mathbb E\int_0^\tau I_s\,ds\leq a_{L_1}$.  Since the hub-reactivation
  1075	intensity is at most $K(I_s+\ell_s)/C$, its probability before pendant
  1076	clearance and ordinary extinction is at most
  1077	$K(m+a_{L_1})/C$.  Conditional bias also gives
  1078	\[
  1079	 \Prob_{\{I\leq L_1\}}(\tau_{L_2}<\tau_0)
  1080	 \leq K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}.
  1081	\]
  1082	If neither event occurs, the process enters $X_{L_2}$.  Restoring the
  1083	suppressed reactivation only adds its displayed probability, so the strong
  1084	Markov property yields
  1085	\begin{equation}
  1086	 y^{\dB}_{C,L}
  1087	 \leq \eta_L(L_1,T)+\frac{K(m+a_{L_1})}{C}
  1088	      +K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}+x^{\dB}_{C,L_2}.
  1089	\label{eq:renewal-y-dB}
  1090	\end{equation}
  1091	For fixed $L_1,L_2,T$, let $C\to\infty$; then let $L_2\to\infty$,
  1092	$L_1\to\infty$, and $T\to\infty$ in that order.  Equations
  1093	\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} prove
  1094	$y^U_{C,L}=o(1)$ for each fixed $L$.
  1095	
  1096	Return now to one ordinary mutant.  At its first hub change, the explicit
  1097	hazard-weighted estimates \eqref{eq:green-macro} and
  1098	\eqref{eq:green-hub-tail} give a constant $a$ such that
  1099	\begin{equation}
  1100	 x^U_{C,1}\leq a e^{-\kappa C}
  1101	 +\frac aC\bigl(y^U_{C,L}+e^{-\theta L}\bigr).
  1102	\label{eq:renewal-refined}
  1103	\end{equation}
  1104	Therefore
  1105	$\limsup_{C\to\infty}Cx^U_{C,1}\leq ae^{-\theta L}$; letting $L\to\infty$
  1106	proves $x^U_{C,1}=o(C^{-1})$, while
  1107	\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} give
  1108	$y^U_{C,0}=o(1)$.  Hence
  1109	\begin{align*}
  1110	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)
  1111	 &\leq\frac{C-1}{C}x^{\Bd}_{C,1}+\frac1Cy^{\Bd}_{C,0}
  1112	 =o(C^{-1}),\\
  1113	 J_H(1/r)
  1114	 &\leq x^{\dB}_{C,1}+\frac{y^{\dB}_{C,0}}{c+m}
  1115	 =o(C^{-1}),
  1116	\end{align*}
  1117	because every ordinary clique vertex has degree $c$ and the hub has degree
  1118	$c+m$.
  1119	\end{proof}
  1120	
  1121	\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:center}]
  1122	Choose $A_0$ so that both $r^{-K}$ and $r_0^{-K}$ in
  1123	Lemmas~\ref{lem:early-establishment} and \ref{lem:core-confinement} are
  1124	$o(q/C)=o(C^{-3/4})$, and then choose $B_0>3/4$ in
  1125	Lemma~\ref{lem:pendant-cleanup}.  Since
  1126	\[
  1127	 K^2/C=o(C^{-3/4}),\qquad C^{-B_0}=o(C^{-3/4}),
  1128	\]
  1129	the early-establishment probability is $p+o(q/C)$ and every established
  1130	family completes fixation with failure $o(q/C)$.  The hub starting vertex
  1131	has normalized mass $C^{-1}=o(q/C)$.  This proves \eqref{eq:core};
  1132	Lemma~\ref{lem:pendant-cleanup} proves \eqref{eq:leaf-Bd} and
  1133	\eqref{eq:leaf-dB}; and Lemma~\ref{lem:reciprocal-renewal} proves the two
  1134	reciprocal estimates.
  1135	
  1136	Finally, average the $C-1$ ordinary starts, the hub start, and the $m$
  1137	pendant starts.  Their contributions give
  1138	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}; the exceptional hub mass is
  1139	$O(C^{-1})=o(q/C)$.
  1140	\end{proof}
  1141	
  1142	\section{Pair gates and the complete post-establishment path}
  1143	
  1144	For the center, let
  1145	\[
  1146	 I_H=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}\frac1{d_x}
  1147	 =1+\frac1{C-1+m},
  1148	\qquad
  1149	 J_H(r)=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}
  1150	 \frac{h_H^{\dB}(\{x\};r)}{d_x},
  1151	\]
  1152	where $h_H^{\dB}$ is the isolated-center dB fixation committor.
  1153	For a pair with internal weight $W=C/\sigma$, put
  1154	\[
  1155	 I_P=\frac2W=\frac{2\sigma}{C},\qquad
  1156	 J_P(r)=\frac1W=\frac{\sigma}{C}.
  1157	\]
  1158	The second identity uses the exact dB singleton value $1/2$ on $K_2$.
  1159	The center estimates imply
  1160	\[
  1161	 I_H=1+o(1),\qquad J_H(r)=p+o(1),
  1162	\]
  1163	while $J_H(1/r)$ and the reciprocal core fixation probability are
  1164	$o(C^{-1})$.
  1165	
  1166	\begin{proposition}[Gate odds and global sweep]
  1167	\label{prop:gate}
  1168	For every fixed $r>1$, the separated macro-fixation probabilities satisfy
  1169	\[
  1170	 P_U^H=1-o(q/C),
  1171	 \qquad
  1172	 P_{\Bd}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_B}{1+Z_B},
  1173	 \qquad
  1174	 P_{\dB}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_D}{1+Z_D},
  1175	\]
  1176	where
  1177	\[
  1178	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1179	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1180	\]
  1181	The convergence is uniform when $r$ ranges over a compact subinterval of
  1182	$(1,\infty)$.
  1183	\end{proposition}
  1184	
  1185	\begin{proof}
  1186	For one specified pair, let $A,D,B,C'$ denote, respectively, mutant-pair
  1187	invasion of a resident center, resident-center recovery of that pair,
  1188	mutant-center invasion of a resident pair, and resident-pair recovery of
  1189	the center.  In each row below $a_P$ means the isolated pair fixation
  1190	$a_P^U$ for that update rule.  Summing the parent--target or death--parent events gives,
  1191	after a common time change,
  1192	\[
  1193	\begin{array}{c|cccc}
  1194	 &A&D&B&C'\\ \hline
  1195	 \Bd&
  1196	 CrI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(r)&
  1197	 2I_Ha_P(1/r)&
  1198	 2rI_Ha_P(r)&
  1199	 CI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(1/r)\\[1mm]
  1200	 \dB&
  1201	 2rJ_H(r)&
  1202	 (C/r)J_P(1/r)&
  1203	 CrJ_P(r)&
  1204	 (2/r)J_H(1/r).
  1205	\end{array}
  1206	\]
  1207	The isolated pair values are
  1208	\[
  1209	 a_P^{\Bd}(r)=\frac r{r+1},\quad
  1210	 a_P^{\Bd}(1/r)=\frac1{r+1},\quad
  1211	 a_P^{\dB}(r)=a_P^{\dB}(1/r)=\frac12.
  1212	\]
  1213	
  1214	On macro states the only nonzero rates are
  1215	\[
  1216	\begin{array}{ll}
  1217	 (0,k)\to(1,k):kA,&(0,k)\to(0,k-1):kD,\\
  1218	 (1,k)\to(1,k+1):(q-k)B,&(1,k)\to(0,k):(q-k)C'.
  1219	\end{array}
  1220	\]
  1221	From $(1,k)$, the chance that the center reverses before the next pair
  1222	conversion is $C'/(B+C')$.  A union bound over at most $q$ conversions gives
  1223	\[
  1224	 1-P_U^H\le q\frac{C'}{B+C'}.
  1225	\]
  1226	Proposition~\ref{prop:center} and the portal estimates give
  1227	$qC'/B=o(q/C)$, uniformly on each fitness compact.  Thus a mutant center
  1228	converts all resident pairs with probability $1-o(q/C)$.
  1229	
  1230	From $(0,1)$, the first successful macro event is either center invasion at
  1231	rate $A$ or loss of the sole mutant pair at rate $D$.  Hence
  1232	\[
  1233	 P_U^P=\frac{A}{A+D}\,p_{1,1},
  1234	\]
  1235	where $p_{1,1}$ is the macro-fixation probability from $(1,1)$.  The same
  1236	sweep bound, now with at most $q-1$ remaining conversions, gives
  1237	$p_{1,1}\to1$.  (Equivalently,
  1238	$P_U^P=[A/(A+D)][(B+C')/B]P_U^H$.)
  1239	The rate table and the isolated $K_2$ values give
  1240	\[
  1241	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1242	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1243	\]
  1244	More precisely, $A/D\to Z_U$, which yields the stated limits.  The argument
  1245	uses the exact absorbing macro chain, including adverse reversals; no
  1246	branching survival probability is substituted for global fixation.
  1247	\end{proof}
  1248	
  1249	\section{The simultaneous correction and its optimization}
  1250	
  1251	Put $\eta=q/C$.  Substitution of
  1252	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB} and
  1253	Proposition~\ref{prop:gate} into the finite trace gives the key asymptotic
  1254	statement.
  1255	
  1256	\begin{proposition}[Two response functions]
  1257	\label{prop:responses}
  1258	For $C=t^4$, $q=t$, $m/q\to\lambda$, and fixed $\sigma>0$, the separated
  1259	trace satisfies
  1260	\begin{align}
  1261	 \frac{\rho^0_{\Bd,t}(r)}{\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1262	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1263	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)}
  1264	 +\frac{\lambda}{r-1}\right]+o_r(\eta),\label{eq:B}\\
  1265	 \frac{\rho^0_{\dB,t}(r)}{\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1266	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1267	 \frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}
  1268	 -\lambda\right]+o_r(\eta).\label{eq:D}
  1269	\end{align}
  1270	The remainders are uniform on every compact subinterval of $(1,\infty)$.
  1271	\end{proposition}
  1272	
  1273	\begin{proof}
  1274	The complete-graph Bd baseline differs from $p$ exponentially in $C$, while
  1275	the dB baseline differs from $p$ by $O(C^{-1})$; both errors are
  1276	$o(\eta)$.  The center terms follow from
  1277	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}.  The pair terms follow by the
  1278	exact simplifications
  1279	\begin{align}
  1280	 2\left\{\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right\}
  1281	 &=\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\\
  1282	 2\left\{\frac{[1/2]Z_D/(1+Z_D)}p-1\right\}
  1283	 &=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1284	\end{align}
  1285	One leaf replaces one core vertex and contributes
  1286	$1/p-1=1/(r-1)$ under Bd and $-1$ under dB.
  1287	All inputs are compact-uniform by Propositions~\ref{prop:weak-trace},
  1288	\ref{prop:center}, and~\ref{prop:gate}, so the combined remainders are as
  1289	stated.
  1290	\end{proof}
  1291	
  1292	\begin{lemma}[Exact tangency]
  1293	\label{lem:tangency}
  1294	At $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$, both response functions in
  1295	\eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} are strictly positive for every
  1296	$1<r<\Rhyb$ and vanish simultaneously at $r=\Rhyb$.
  1297	\end{lemma}
  1298	
  1299	\begin{proof}
  1300	Both bracketed corrections are positive precisely when
  1301	\[
  1302	 L(r,\sigma)<\lambda<U(r,\sigma),
  1303	\]
  1304	where
  1305	\[
  1306	 L=\frac{2(1-\sigma)(r-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\qquad
  1307	 U=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1308	\]
  1309	After multiplication by positive denominators, $L<U$ is equivalent to
  1310	\[
  1311	 F_r(\sigma)<0,
  1312	\]
  1313	where
  1314	\[
  1315	 F_r(\sigma)
  1316	 =(r-1)\sigma^2+(r^3-4r^2+3r+1)\sigma+r(2r-3).
  1317	\]
  1318	Its minimizing value and minimum are
  1319	\[
  1320	 \sigma(r)=\frac{-r^3+4r^2-3r-1}{2(r-1)},\qquad
  1321	 \min_\sigma F_r(\sigma)=-\frac{P(r)}{4(r-1)}.
  1322	\]
  1323	Exact Sturm counting gives no root of $P$ in $(1,3/2)$ and exactly one in
  1324	$(3/2,151/100)$.  At that root, $\sigma_*$ is the double tangency and
  1325	\[
  1326	 L(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=U(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=\lambda_*.
  1327	\]
  1328	For fixed $\sigma_*$, $L$ is increasing and $U$ decreasing on
  1329	$(1,\Rhyb)$ because
  1330	\[
  1331	 \partial_rL=
  1332	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)\{\sigma(r-1)^2-1\}}
  1333	      {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}^2}>0,\qquad
  1334	 \partial_rU=
  1335	 \frac{4r(\sigma-r)}{\{2r(r-1)+\sigma\}^2}<0.
  1336	\]
  1337	Here $0<\sigma_*<1<r$ and
  1338	$\sigma_*(r-1)^2<1$ throughout the isolating interval, so the displayed
  1339	factorizations determine the signs without numerical approximation.
  1340	Thus
  1341	\[
  1342	 L(r,\sigma_*)<\lambda_*<U(r,\sigma_*)
  1343	 \quad(1<r<\Rhyb),
  1344	\]
  1345	which proves the claim.
  1346	\end{proof}
  1347	
  1348	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
  1349	Fix $r\in(1,\Rhyb)$.  Then $r\in I_t$ for every sufficiently large $t$.
  1350	By Proposition~\ref{prop:responses} and Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}, the two
  1351	separated normalized gains, after multiplication by $n_t/q_t$, converge to
  1352	strictly positive numbers.  The defining inequality for $e_t$ changes each
  1353	scaled gain by at most $1/t$.  Thus both actual connected-graph gains are
  1354	strictly positive for all sufficiently large $t$.  The sequence, including
  1355	every weak-edge weight, was fixed before $r$ was chosen, which is exactly the
  1356	quantifier order in the definition of $\Rsim$.
  1357	\end{proof}
  1358	
  1359	\begin{proposition}[Fixed-parameter response optimality]
  1360	\label{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1361	Among fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute
  1362	pair--pendant response model \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D}, $\Rhyb$ is the exact
  1363	largest threshold of an interval beginning at one on which both corrections
  1364	can remain positive.
  1365	\end{proposition}
  1366	
  1367	\begin{proof}
  1368	For fixed positive $\sigma$, feasibility implies $F_r(\sigma)<0$, and hence
  1369	$\min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)<0$.  At $\Rhyb$ the unconstrained minimum is
  1370	zero at the admissible value $0<\sigma_*<1$.  Exact evaluation gives
  1371	\[
  1372	 P(3/2)=\frac1{64}>0,\qquad
  1373	 P(151/100)=-\frac{39866792399}{10^{12}}<0.
  1374	\]
  1375	The Sturm count in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency} shows that $\Rhyb$ is the only
  1376	root between these endpoints.  Hence $P(r)<0$ on
  1377	$(\Rhyb,151/100]$ and
  1378	$\min_sF_r(s)=-P(r)/\{4(r-1)\}>0$ there.  No positive $\sigma$---indeed no
  1379	real $\sigma$---is feasible immediately beyond $\Rhyb$.  Since the fixed
  1380	pair $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$ works throughout $(1,\Rhyb)$, no interval
  1381	beginning at one can extend farther within this response model.
  1382	\end{proof}
  1383	
  1384	\begin{corollary}[A rational-edge family]
  1385	\label{cor:rational}
  1386	Taking $\sigma=19/137$ and $\lambda=20/27$ gives, exactly at $r=3/2$,
  1387	\[
  1388	 B=\frac{232}{17361}>0,\qquad
  1389	 D=\frac{65}{12123}>0.
  1390	\]
  1391	This choice gives a family with entirely rational edge weights and algebraic
  1392	response threshold
  1393	\[
  1394	 R_{\mathbb Q}
  1395	 =\frac{5069+12\sqrt{147001}}{6439}
  1396	 =1.50176815223369\ldots>3/2.
  1397	\]
  1398	Thus a family with rational internal ratios and rational positive weak edges
  1399	already proves a strict interval beyond $3/2$.
  1400	\end{corollary}
  1401	
  1402	\begin{proof}
  1403	Substitution into \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} gives the two displayed
  1404	margins.  For these rational parameters the dB response first vanishes at
  1405	the unique root in $(1,2)$ of
  1406	\[
  1407	 6439r^2-10138r+703=0,
  1408	\]
  1409	which is $R_{\mathbb Q}$; the Bd response is still strictly positive there.
  1410	Both responses are therefore positive on $(1,R_{\mathbb Q})$ by the same
  1411	monotonicity calculation used in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}.  The weak edges
  1412	remain rational as well: repeat the least-dyadic diagonal construction with
  1413	$I_t=[1+1/t,R_{\mathbb Q}-1/t]$, $\sigma=19/137$, and
  1414	$m_t=\lfloor(20/27)t\rfloor$.  Every edge-weight ratio in this specialized
  1415	family is therefore rational.
  1416	\end{proof}
  1417	
  1418	\section{Discussion}
  1419	
  1420	The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.  One
  1421	weighted graph sequence is chosen without knowing fitness and works for each
  1422	fixed $r$ in an interval extending beyond $3/2$.  A family tuned separately
  1423	at each fitness would establish a weaker statement.  The diagonal choice of
  1424	$\eps_t$ is therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical
  1425	limit: compact convergence selects one positive cut that works
  1426	simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
  1427	
  1428	The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures.  Heavy
  1429	pairs contribute positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub
  1430	pendants contribute positively to Bd and negatively to dB.  Their densities
  1431	are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and the module
  1432	responses appear at the common scale $q_t/C_t$.  The exact Schur trace is
  1433	important here.  It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut
  1434	event and retains the possibility that a nearly successful global sweep
  1435	reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would not
  1436	prove fixation.
  1437	
  1438	Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be
  1439	resolved: reproduction can select a parent before a replacement site, or a
  1440	vacancy can first determine which neighbors compete.  The construction
  1441	shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
  1442	occupy a special environment.  A vanishing fraction of paired niches and
  1443	hub-associated peripheral sites can bias invasion in complementary ways
  1444	while the bulk remains well mixed.  The result is qualitative rather than a
  1445	claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
  1446	structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this
  1447	beneficial-fitness regime.
  1448	
  1449	There are two sharp limitations.  Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1450	optimizes only fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed
  1451	first-order dilute response model.  It does not classify size-dependent or
  1452	singular choices $\sigma_t,\lambda_t$, different pair or pendant density
  1453	scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order boundary, nonseparated
  1454	pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions.  Conversely, the
  1455	paper supplies no finite universal upper bound on $\Rsim$.  Establishing
  1456	such a bound would require a comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on
  1457	arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a classification of this
  1458	construction.
  1459	
  1460	The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small.  Here
  1461	$q_t/C_t=t^{-3}=\Theta(n_t^{-3/4})$, so the normalized fixation gain tends to
  1462	zero.  The pair weights grow like $C_t$, while the exact diagonal may select
  1463	very small positive weak edges.  The proof gives eventual positivity for
  1464	each fixed fitness but no useful finite-size bound on $t_0(r)$, and it makes
  1465	no claim of empirical or finite-population optimality.
  1466	
  1467	\section*{Statements and Declarations}
  1468	
  1469	\noindent\textbf{Data and code availability.}
  1470	No empirical data are used.  The LaTeX source, exact symbolic and rational
  1471	verifiers, labelled-chain audit, and pinned build and replay scripts accompany
  1472	this revision as the deterministic Supplementary Material archive
  1473	\nolinkurl{simultaneous_amplifier_beyond_three_halves_source_and_certificates.tar.gz}.
  1474	It contains an internal SHA-256 manifest and a fresh-environment bootstrap.
  1475	The corresponding development sources are in the
  1476	\href{https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/simultaneous-amplification-beyond-three-halves-v2.0.1/universal_simultaneous_amplification/phase4_landmark_closure/paper_hybrid_threshold}{paper-specific frozen repository directory}.
  1477	The full-repository software snapshot at
  1478	\href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21852072}{doi:10.5281/zenodo.21852072}
  1479	contains the unrefereed v1
  1480	manuscript, source, and software, not this superseding revision.  No new
  1481	persistent identifier has yet been assigned.  When this revision is posted
  1482	as a preprint, the same frozen bundle will be deposited alongside it and this
  1483	statement updated with the assigned identifier.
  1484	The replay enumerates all $512$ configurations and $108$ orbit fibres of its
  1485	finite audit graph and independently checks the response algebra, rational
  1486	margins, Sturm isolation, and tangency.  These certificates audit finite
  1487	transition aggregation and exact algebra; the weak-cut and population
  1488	asymptotics are analytic proofs in the manuscript, not computer-assisted
  1489	consequences of the replay.
