   689	\[
   690	 p_0=\frac{H_+}{H_++V_\ell}p_1,\qquad
   691	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell}{H_-+U_\ell}
   692	     +\frac{H_-}{H_-+U_\ell}p_0.
   693	\]
   694	To make the interruption comparison formal, replace these rates by
   695	\[
   696	 \underline H_+=r_-(1-2\delta+\ell),\qquad
   697	 \overline H_-=2\delta+m-\ell,
   698	 \qquad \underline U_\ell=\frac{r_-(m-\ell)}{c+m},
   699	\]
   700	and let $(\underline p_0,\underline p_1)$ solve the same two equations with
   701	these worst rates and $V_\ell$.  The resulting two-state hitting function is
   702	subharmonic for the full $(h,i,\ell)$ generator until the next pendant-count
   703	change: core jumps leave it unchanged, while
   704	$H_+\geq\underline H_+$, $H_-\leq\overline H_-$, and
   705	$U_\ell\geq\underline U_\ell$ move its generator in the favorable direction.
   706	Indeed, the two renewal equations give
   707	\[
   708	 p_0=\frac{H_+U_\ell}
   709	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)},\qquad
   710	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell(H_++V_\ell)}
   711	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)};
   712	\]
   713	direct differentiation shows that both are increasing in $H_+$ and
   714	$U_\ell$ and decreasing in $H_-$.  This proves the asserted favorable
   715	monotonicity rather than leaving it implicit.
   716	Optional stopping therefore makes it a lower bound even with an adapted
   717	sequence of core interruptions.  Its next-leaf up/down odds, from either hub
   718	phase, are bounded below by
   719	\begin{equation}
   720	 \frac{\underline H_+\underline U_\ell}
   721	      {\overline H_-V_\ell}
   722	 \frac1{1+\underline U_\ell/\overline H_-}
   723	 \geq\frac{r_-^2}{1+2\delta}\{1-o(1)\}>1.
   724	\label{eq:leaf-trace-odds}
   725	\end{equation}
   726	for $1\leq\ell<m$.
   727	
   728	The calendar-time estimate can be checked without suppressing core events.
   729	During an $h=0$ phase the activation-or-loss intensity is bounded below by a
   730	positive constant, and during an $h=1$ phase the deactivation-or-gain
   731	intensity is at least $m-\ell\geq1$.  Moreover,
   732	\begin{align*}
   733	 \Prob(\text{loss before activation}\mid h=0)
   734	 &\geq \frac{V_\ell}{r_+(1+\ell)+V_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},\\
   735	 \Prob(\text{gain before deactivation}\mid h=1)
   736	 &\geq\frac{\underline U_\ell}
   737	 {2\delta+m-\ell+\underline U_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C}.
   738	\end{align*}
   739	Core jumps contribute zero to functions of $(h,\ell)$ and do not pause these
   740	competing clocks.  Hence the number of hub phases before the next pendant
   741	change is dominated by a geometric variable of mean $O(C)$, and each phase
   742	has uniformly bounded mean duration.  Thus
   743	\begin{equation}
   744	 \mathbb E(\sigma_{j+1}-\sigma_j\mid\mathcal F_{\sigma_j})
   745	 \leq K_0C,
   746	\label{eq:leaf-wait}
   747	\end{equation}
   748	where $\sigma_j$ are successive pendant-count changes.
   749	Thus the pendant trace is dominated below by a reflected walk whose
   750	up/down odds are at least a fixed $\beta>1$.  It makes $O(m)$ expected
   751	changes before reaching $m$; together with \eqref{eq:leaf-wait},
   752	\begin{equation}
   753	 \sup_{R\leq2\delta c}\mathbb E\tau_{\{\ell=m\}}=O(Cm).
   754	\label{eq:leaf-hitting-time}
   755	\end{equation}
   756	
   757	At $\ell=m$, a resident hub activates before the next pendant loss with
   758	probability $1-O(C^{-1})$.  After activation, first suppress hub loss and
   759	keep $\ell=m$.  The Bd deficit generator is then exactly
   760	\[
   761	 \mathcal G R
   762	 =-R\left\{\frac r{c+m}+(r-1)\left(1-\frac Rc\right)\right\}.
   763	\]
   764	After shrinking $\delta$ once, the bracket is at least a compact-uniform
   765	$\kappa>0$ on $R\leq3\delta c$.  Starting from $R\leq2\delta c$ and stopping
   766	at $\tau=\tau_0\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}$, Dynkin's formula gives
   767	\[
   768	 \mathbb E\int_0^\tau R_s\,ds\leq\frac{R(0)}\kappa
   769	 \leq\frac{2\delta c}\kappa.
   770	\]
   771	The same adapted step calculation, now with $3\delta$ in place of
   772	$2\delta$, also gives
   773	$\Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}<\tau_0)\leq e^{-\gamma C}$.  Conditional
   774	on the suppressed path, the probability of no hub loss is
   775	$\exp\{-\int_0^\tau R_s/c\,ds\}$.  Jensen's inequality and subtraction of
   776	the upper-exit event therefore yield
   777	\begin{equation}
   778	 \Prob(\hbox{no hub loss and }\tau_0<\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}})
   779	 \geq e^{-2\delta/\kappa}-e^{-\gamma C}=:s_1+o(1)>0.
   780	\label{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}
   781	\end{equation}
   782	The same exponential Lyapunov estimate makes this final cleanup occur within
   783	$C^2$ with probability $1-o(1)$.  Together with
   784	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time} and $m=o(C)$, Markov's inequality gives a fixed
   785	$s_0>0$ chance of full cleanup in a block of $2C^2$ time.  If hub loss ends
   786	an unsuccessful final attempt, the same adapted deficit comparison, now
   787	using the nested strips $2\delta c<3\delta c<4\delta c$ after shrinking
   788	$\delta$, returns the process to $R\leq\delta c$ in polynomial time before
   789	the next block; escape to $4\delta c$ before that return costs
   790	$e^{-\gamma C}$.  Starting the next block from $R\leq\delta c$ puts it back
   791	under the $2\delta c$ confinement estimate.  The earlier
   792	hit of $3\delta c$ in \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup} is charged to this same
   793	exceptional event rather than recycled as a new block.  We now formalize the
   794	repetition.
   795	
   796	Here is the stopping-time regeneration behind that last sentence.  Put
   797	$\mathcal E_C=\{R\leq\delta c\}$, write $\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}$ for the
   798	fixation time, and let $S_1$ be the initial time (or the first entry into
   799	$\mathcal E_C$).  Given $S_j$, run the following block:
   800	put
   801	\[
   802	 \Sigma_j=\inf\{s\geq S_j:(h_s,\ell_s)=(1,m)
   803	                         \text{ or }R_s\geq2\delta c\}.
   804	\]
   805	At each visit to $\ell=m$ with resident hub, activation precedes the next
   806	pendant loss with probability $1-O(C^{-1})$; after a loss the same pendant
   807	trace restarts.  Thus \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time} also gives
   808	$\mathbb E(\Sigma_j-S_j\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})\leq K C m$, with a hit of
   809	$2\delta c$ charged as exceptional.  From a nonexceptional $\Sigma_j$, run
   810	the final cleanup until fixation, hub loss, or a hit of $3\delta c$;
   811	and, after an ordinary hub-loss failure below $3\delta c$, wait for the
   812	first return to $\mathcal E_C$, charging a hit of $4\delta c$ as exceptional.
   813	Let $T_j$ be the time of fixation, exceptional escape, or this return.  On
   814	an ordinary return set $S_{j+1}=T_j$; a hit of $3\delta c$ is charged rather
   815	than recycled.  For that return, shrink $\delta$ so that
   816	$\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ on $\delta c<R<4\delta c$, and put
   817	$\rho=\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}}\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}$.
   818	The same adapted-step and generator arguments give, from $R<3\delta c$,
   819	\[
   820	 \Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}<\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}})
   821	 \leq e^{-\gamma C},\qquad
   822	 \Prob(\rho>t)\leq3e^{-\kappa t}.
   823	\]
   824	Equations
   825	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}, \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}, and
   826	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}, together with this nested-strip estimate, give
   827	constants $s_*>0$, $D_*=D_*(B_0)$, and $\gamma_*>0$ such that, uniformly on
   828	$\mathcal F_{S_j}$,
   829	\begin{align}
   830	 \Prob(\hbox{fixation during block }j,\ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}
   831	       \mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   832	 &\geq s_*,\label{eq:Bd-block-success}\\
   833	 \Prob(\hbox{upper-strip escape during block }j,
   834	       \ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   835	 &\leq K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C},\label{eq:Bd-block-escape}\\
   836	 \Prob(T_j-S_j>C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   837	 &\leq C^{-B_0-2}.
   838	 \label{eq:Bd-block-duration}
   839	\end{align}
   840	For the last bound, the synchronization part has conditional mean $O(Cm)$
   841	by \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}; the final attempt and return part has
   842	conditional mean $O(C^2)$ by the stopped Lyapunov estimates.  Markov's
   843	inequality gives \eqref{eq:Bd-block-duration} after taking, for example,
   844	$D_*=B_0+4$.  With
   845	\[
   846	 N_C=\left\lceil
   847	 \frac{(B_0+2)\log C}{-\log(1-s_*)}\right\rceil,
   848	\]
   849	the strong Markov property at the $S_j$ gives
   850	\[
   851	 \Prob(\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}>N_CC^{D_*})
   852	 \leq(1-s_*)^{N_C}
   853	   +N_C\{C^{-B_0-2}+K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C}\}
   854	 =O(C^{-B_0}).
   855	\]
   856	Moreover $N_CC^{D_*}\leq C^{D_{\rm cl}}$ for a fixed
   857	$D_{\rm cl}=D_{\rm cl}(B_0)$.  This proves the
   858	claimed Bd time and failure bounds without an implicit independence
   859	assumption between blocks.
   860	
   861	Under dB, every pendant copies the hub at its next death.  In the bounded
   862	$R$ strip, first wait for $R\leq R_0$ using
   863	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}.  The hub activation rate is then bounded below.
   864	During a mutant-hub interval, its deactivation intensity is at most
   865	$K(R+m-\ell)/C$.  Suppressing that deactivation, direct substitution in the
   866	dB table gives $\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ while $R\leq2\delta c$, and the
   867	number $m-\ell$ of resident pendants is a pure-death chain with drift
   868	$-(m-\ell)$.  Choose a fixed $\beta_0=\beta_0(B_0)>0$ such that
   869	\[
   870	 \beta_0-\frac14\geq B_0+2,
   871	 \qquad
   872	 \kappa\beta_0\geq B_0+2,
   873	\]
   874	and put $T=\beta_0\log C$.  Then
   875	\begin{align*}
   876	 \Prob(\ell_T<m)&\leq me^{-T},\\
   877	 \Prob(R_T>0,\ \tau_\uparrow>T)
   878	 &\leq R_0e^{-\kappa T},\\
   879	 \Prob(\text{hub deactivates before }T\wedge\tau_\uparrow)
   880	 &\leq \mathbb E\int_0^{T\wedge\tau_\uparrow}
   881	 K\frac{R_s+m-\ell_s}{C}\,ds
   882	   \leq\frac K C\left(\frac{R_0}{\kappa}+m\right)=o(1).
   883	\end{align*}
   884	Here $\tau_\uparrow=\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}$; its omitted upper-strip exit
   885	has probability $e^{-\gamma C}$.  Since $m=O(C^{1/4})$, the choice of
   886	$\beta_0$ makes the first two displayed probabilities $O(C^{-B_0-2})$,
   887	whereas hub survival has probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus an activated attempt
   888	started in $R\leq R_0$ succeeds with
   889	probability at least $1/2$ for large $C$.  After a failed attempt, confinement
   890	and \eqref{eq:deficit-return} return the process to $R\leq R_0$ in
   891	polynomial time; unless fixation has already occurred, the hub is activated
   892	again at a rate bounded below.  The strong Markov property over
   893	$O_{B_0}(\log C)$ such attempts gives failure $O(C^{-B_0})$, while their
   894	total duration is polynomial.  Taking $D_{\rm cl}$ larger than the two block
   895	exponents and using \eqref{eq:core-confinement} proves the first assertion.
   896	
   897	For a Bd mutant leaf, use the standard weighted graphical construction: each
   898	vertex $u$ has a base reproduction clock of rate one and, when it rings,
   899	targets $v$ with probability $w_{uv}/d_u$; a mutant $u$ has an independent
   900	extra clock of rate $r-1$ with the same target law.  Deleting mutants can
   901	only decrease the mutant set.  Fix the original mutant pendant.  At every
   902	regeneration with resident hub, no ordinary mutants, and that pendant still
   903	alive, delete all other mutants and suppress births to the other pendants.
   904	This produces the adverse lower state $(h,i,\ell)=(0,0,1)$.
   905	In its resident-hub phase, activation has rate $r$ and loss of the
   906	distinguished pendant has rate $1/d$, so loss before activation is
   907	$O(C^{-1})$.  In the ensuing mutant-hub phase, an ordinary seed has rate at
   908	least $rc/d\geq a_0$, while hub loss has rate $m$; hence a seed precedes hub
   909	loss with probability at least $a_1/m$.
   910	
   911	After a seed, stop the ordinary count on $\{0,K\}$.  For every hidden hub
   912	and pendant state, the Bd up/down odds on $1\leq i<K$ are at least a fixed
   913	$r_1>1$.  The conditional-bias argument therefore gives
   914	$\Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)\geq p_0>0$.  The embedded walk makes $O(K)$ expected
   915	changes, and its changing intensity is bounded below, so
   916	$\mathbb E\tau_{0,K}=O(K)$.  The distinguished-pendant loss intensity is at
   917	most $1/d$, giving $O(K/C)$ loss during this stopped trial.  If the count
   918	hits zero while the pendant survives, wait for resident-hub regeneration,
   919	suppressing or deleting every ordinary reseed and every additional mutant
   920	pendant until hub loss.  This is again an adverse graphical coupling: the
   921	distinguished pendant cannot be lost while its sole neighbor is mutant, and
   922	any subsequent resident-phase loss is charged as trial failure.  Delete the
   923	extra mutants again at regeneration.  If it hits $K$, the preceding completion
   924	argument fixes with probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus a trial succeeds with
   925	probability at least $a_2/m$, whereas it loses the distinguished pendant
   926	with probability $O((1+K)/C)$.  The elementary renewal inequality for these
   927	competing outcomes gives loss before success
   928	$O(m(1+K)/C)=o(1)$.  Hence $u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1)$.
   929	Under dB, the leaf dies at rate one after the common death-clock time change,
   930	whereas hub activation is at most $r_+/C$.  Fixation requires that activation,
   931	so $u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1})$.
   932	\end{proof}
   933	
   934	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal killed-Green bounds]
   935	\label{lem:killed-green}
   936	Put $s=1/r$ and suppress hub changes while $h=0,\ell=0$.  Stop the resulting
   937	ordinary-count chain $I$ on hitting $0$ or $\lceil\delta c\rceil$, and define
   938	\[
   939	 G_C^U(i,j)=\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
   940	             \mathbf1_{\{I_z=j\}}\,dz.
   941	\]
   942	For every fixed $L$ there are $a_L,\kappa,\theta>0$, uniform for
   943	$s\in[1/r_+,1/r_-]$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$, such that
   944	\begin{align}
   945	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i(\tau_{\lceil\delta c\rceil}<\tau_0)
   946	 &\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C},\label{eq:green-macro}\\
   947	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_jjG_C^U(i,j)&\leq a_L,
   948	 \label{eq:green-mass}\\
   949	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_{j>L'}jG_C^U(i,j)
   950	 &\leq a_Le^{-\theta L'}.
   951	 \label{eq:green-tail}
   952	\end{align}
   953	Consequently the probability of a hub change before ordinary extinction is
   954	$O_L(C^{-1})$, and the ordinary count at that change has an exponentially
   955	decreasing tail after multiplication by $C$.
   956	\end{lemma}
   957	
   958	\begin{proof}
   959	For $i\leq\delta c$, the reciprocal-fitness versions of the two rate tables
   960	give
   961	\[
   962	 \frac{q_i^+}{q_i^-}\leq s_+\{1+O(\delta)+O(C^{-1})\}\leq\vartheta<1
   963	\]
   964	after decreasing $\delta$, where $s_+=1/r_-$.  They also give
   965	$q_i^--q_i^+\geq\kappa_0i$ in the displayed clock.  The product-odds formula
   966	for the dominated nearest-neighbor walk proves \eqref{eq:green-macro}.
   967	Dynkin's formula for $I_{z\wedge\tau}$ gives
   968	\[
   969	 \kappa_0\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_z\,dz\leq i,
   970	\]
   971	which is \eqref{eq:green-mass}.  To obtain \eqref{eq:green-tail}, let $\nu$
   972	be the first hit of $L'+1$.  Conditional bias gives
   973	$\Prob_i(\nu<\tau_0)\leq K_L\vartheta^{L'-L}$.  After $\nu$, the same Dynkin
   974	estimate bounds the expected remaining particle-time by
   975	$(L'+1)/\kappa_0$.  Therefore
   976	\[
   977	 \mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
   978	 \leq \frac{K_L(L'+1)}{\kappa_0}\vartheta^{L'-L}
   979	 \leq a_Le^{-\theta L'},
   980	\]
